        {
          "name": "Mathematics",
          "description": "Sets, functions, financial mathematics, geometry and statistics",
          "units": [
            {
              "name": "Unit 1: Sets of Numbers",
              "previous_answers": "",
              "lessons": [
                "Natural numbers and integers",
                "Rational numbers",
                "Operations on numbers",
                "Number line and ordering",
                "Applications of numbers in daily life"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: identify natural numbers and distinguish them from integers, represent integers on a number line, and perform simple comparisons of natural numbers and integers.",
                  "reading": "Welcome to our first lesson on sets of numbers! Today, we'll explore two important sets: Natural Numbers and Integers.  Natural numbers are the counting numbers we use every day. Think about counting the number of students in your classroom in Kigali, or the number of cows your family owns: 1, 2, 3, and so on, going on forever.  We can write this set as {1, 2, 3, 4, ...}.  Integers are a larger set that includes natural numbers, their negative counterparts, and zero.  So, integers include ..., -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, ... . Think about temperature in Musanze: if it's 5 degrees Celsius, that's a positive integer. If it drops to -2 degrees Celsius, that's a negative integer. Zero represents a point of balance, like having no Rwandan francs in your pocket.  Zero is an integer but not a natural number.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: List the first five natural numbers.\nSolution:\nNatural numbers are the counting numbers starting from 1. So, the first five natural numbers are 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.\n\nExample 2: Place the integers -3, 0, and 2 on a number line.\nSolution:\nDraw a line and mark points at equal intervals. Label the middle point as 0. Numbers to the right of 0 are positive, and numbers to the left are negative.  Mark -3 to the left of 0, and 2 to the right of 0.\n\n    <---o-----o-----o-----o-----o--->\n       -3    -2    -1     0     1     2",
                  "practice": "1. Which of the following are natural numbers: 7, -4, 0, 15, -2.5?\n2. Write down the integer that is immediately to the left of -5 on a number line.\n3. Is 100 a natural number? Is it an integer? Explain your answer.\n4. Arrange the following integers in ascending order: -8, 3, 0, -1, 5.\n5. Imagine you have 10,000 Rwandan francs and you spend 12,000 Rwandan francs on a new mobile phone. Represent the amount of money you have left as an integer.",
                  "summary": "* Natural numbers are the counting numbers: {1, 2, 3, ...}\n* Integers include natural numbers, their negative counterparts, and zero: {..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ...}\n* Zero is an integer but not a natural number.\n* Integers can be represented on a number line, with negative numbers to the left of zero and positive numbers to the right.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Which of the following statements is TRUE?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "All integers are natural numbers.",
                      "B": "Natural numbers include zero.",
                      "C": "Zero is an integer.",
                      "D": "Negative numbers are natural numbers."
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 7, 15\n2. -6\n3. Yes, both\n4. -8, -1, 0, 3, 5\n5. -2,000 RWF"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Define what a rational number is.\n- Identify and represent rational numbers on a number line.\n- Convert between fractions, decimals, and whole numbers that represent rational numbers.",
                  "reading": "Welcome to our lesson on Rational Numbers! You've already learned about whole numbers and integers. Today, we'll expand our understanding to include rational numbers.  A rational number is any number that can be written as a fraction p/q, where 'p' is an integer (a whole number or its negative) and 'q' is a non-zero integer. Think about dividing Rwandan Francs (RWF) – if you have 100 RWF and want to share it equally among 4 friends, each friend gets 100/4 = 25 RWF. This is a rational number.  Also, terminating decimals (like 0.5 or 3.25) and repeating decimals (like 0.333...) are rational numbers. Integers themselves are rational because they can be written as a fraction with a denominator of 1 (e.g., 5 = 5/1).",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Is the number 7 a rational number? \nSolution: Yes, 7 is a rational number because it can be written as the fraction 7/1. Here, p=7 and q=1, and both are integers, with q being non-zero.\n\nExample 2: Convert the decimal 0.75 to a fraction.\nSolution:  We can write 0.75 as 'seventy-five hundredths', which is 75/100. We can then simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and denominator by their greatest common divisor, which is 25. So, 75/100 = (75 ÷ 25) / (100 ÷ 25) = 3/4. Therefore, 0.75 is a rational number equal to 3/4.",
                  "practice": "1. Is the number -3 a rational number? Explain why or why not.\n2. Write the fraction 2/5 as a decimal.\n3. Represent the number 1.5 on a number line.\n4. Convert the mixed number 2 1/3 to an improper fraction.\n5. A basket contains 30 mangoes. If 12 mangoes are ripe, what fraction of the mangoes are ripe? Express this as a decimal.",
                  "summary": "*   A rational number can be expressed as a fraction p/q where p and q are integers and q is not zero.\n*   Terminating and repeating decimals are rational numbers.\n*   Integers are rational numbers.\n*   Rational numbers can be represented on a number line.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Which of the following is NOT a rational number?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.5",
                      "B": "1/3",
                      "C": "-8",
                      "D": "√2"
                    },
                    "answer": "D"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Yes, -3/1\n2. 0.4\n3. Between 1 and 2\n4. 7/3\n5. 12/30 = 2/5 = 0.4"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n1. Perform addition and subtraction of whole numbers, integers, and decimals.\n2. Perform multiplication and division of whole numbers, integers, and decimals.\n3. Solve word problems involving operations on different types of numbers.",
                  "reading": "In mathematics, we use operations like addition (+), subtraction (-), multiplication (x or *), and division (/ or ÷) to combine or separate numbers.  These operations are fundamental and apply to various sets of numbers we've learned about: whole numbers, integers, and decimals. When adding or subtracting numbers with different signs, remember that integers change the result (e.g., adding a negative number is the same as subtracting its positive counterpart). Multiplication and division follow specific rules for signs: positive times positive is positive, negative times negative is positive, and positive times negative is negative. The same applies to division. For example, imagine you have 5,000 Rwandan Francs (RWF) and you earn another 2,500 RWF, then spend 1,000 RWF, your total is 5,000 + 2,500 - 1,000 = 6,500 RWF. This shows how operations help us manage quantities.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Calculate: 15.7 + (-3.2) - 8.5\nStep 1: Combine the addition and subtraction of the positive numbers: 15.7 - 8.5 = 7.2\nStep 2: Now add the result to the negative number (which is the same as subtracting the positive version): 7.2 + (-3.2) = 7.2 - 3.2 = 4.0\nAnswer: 4.0\n\nExample 2: A group of 4 friends from Kigali are sharing the cost of a bus ticket to Rubavu. The total cost is 12,400 RWF. If one friend pays 3,500 RWF, how much does each of the remaining friends need to pay if they share the rest equally?\nStep 1: Find the remaining cost: 12,400 RWF - 3,500 RWF = 8,900 RWF\nStep 2: Divide the remaining cost among the 3 friends: 8,900 RWF / 3 = 2,966.67 RWF (approximately)\nAnswer: Each of the remaining friends needs to pay approximately 2,966.67 RWF.",
                  "practice": "1. Calculate: 25 + (-10) - 7\n2. Multiply: -6 * 5\n3. Divide: -45 / -9\n4. Sarah bought 3 kg of potatoes at 500 RWF per kg and 2 kg of onions at 750 RWF per kg. How much did she spend in total?\n5. A farmer has 1,500 mangoes. He sells 3/5 of them and then his neighbor gives him 250 more mangoes. How many mangoes does the farmer have now?",
                  "summary": "* Operations like addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division are used to combine or separate numbers.\n* Pay close attention to the signs of integers when performing operations.\n* Multiplication and division have specific rules for how signs affect the result.\n* These operations are used in real-life situations, such as managing money or quantities.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A market vendor in Nyabugogo started the day with 80,000 RWF in her cash box. She sold goods worth 55,500 RWF and then bought new stock for 32,000 RWF. How much money does she have in her cash box at the end of the day?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "103,500 RWF",
                      "B": "24,500 RWF",
                      "C": "135,500 RWF",
                      "D": "32,000 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 8\n2. -30\n3. 5\n4. 3,000 RWF\n5. 850 mangoes"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what a number line is and how to represent numbers on it.\n- Compare and order integers and simple fractions/decimals using a number line.\n- Place Rwandan Franc values on a number line.",
                  "reading": "A **number line** is a straight line with numbers placed at equal intervals along its length. It's like a ruler for numbers! We usually draw a horizontal line with an arrow at each end to show it continues infinitely in both directions. The number zero (0) is usually in the middle. Positive numbers are to the right of zero, and negative numbers are to the left.  For example, imagine you are tracking the daily temperature in Kigali over a week. On Monday it was 25°C, Tuesday 28°C, Wednesday 22°C, and Thursday 20°C. You could place these temperatures on a number line, with 20°C furthest to the left (smallest) and 28°C furthest to the right (largest). This helps us easily see which day was hottest or coldest.",
                  "examples": "Example 1:\nDraw a number line and mark the numbers -3, 0, 2, and 5.\n\nSolution:\n1. Draw a horizontal line.\n2. Mark the center with 0.\n3. Mark equal intervals to the right of 0 for positive numbers: 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.\n4. Mark equal intervals to the left of 0 for negative numbers: -1, -2, -3.\n5. Circle or place a dot on -3, 0, 2, and 5.\n\nExample 2:\nOrder the following amounts of Rwandan Francs from least to greatest on a number line: 1000 Frw, -500 Frw, 0 Frw, 2500 Frw.\n\nSolution:\n1. Draw a number line that includes negative and positive values.\n2. Place 0 in the center.\n3. Mark -500 Frw to the left of 0.\n4. Mark 1000 Frw and 2500 Frw to the right of 0, ensuring 1000 Frw is to the left of 2500 Frw.\n5. The order from least to greatest on the number line is -500 Frw, 0 Frw, 1000 Frw, 2500 Frw.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Place the numbers -5, 1, 4, and -2 on a number line.\n2. (Easy) Which number is greater: 3 or -1? Use a number line to explain.\n3. (Medium) Order the following numbers from least to greatest: -10, 5, -3, 0, 12.\n4. (Medium) On a number line, place the following prices of Rwandan coffee beans: 5000 Frw, 2000 Frw, 8000 Frw.\n5. (Hard) Place the fractions 1/2, -1/4, 3/4, and -3/4 on a number line and order them from least to greatest.",
                  "summary": "* A number line is a visual tool to represent and compare numbers.\n* Positive numbers are to the right of zero, and negative numbers are to the left.\n* Numbers further to the right on the number line are greater.\n* Number lines can be used to order integers, fractions, and decimals.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Which of the following numbers is located furthest to the left on a number line?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "-10",
                      "B": "5",
                      "C": "0",
                      "D": "-2"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. On line: -5, -2, 1, 4 left to right\n2. 3 is greater\n3. -10, -3, 0, 5, 12\n4. 2,000, 5,000, 8,000\n5. -3/4, -1/4, 1/2, 3/4"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: identify different types of numbers used in everyday situations; apply basic arithmetic operations using numbers found in daily life contexts; solve simple real-world problems involving numbers.",
                  "reading": "Numbers are all around us, making our lives easier! We use them for counting, measuring, and making decisions. In Rwanda, you see numbers everywhere: when you buy 'ibigurageza' (snacks) for 500 Rwandan francs (RWF), when you check the time on your watch to catch the bus from Kigali to Huye, or when you count the students in your classroom.  We use natural numbers (1, 2, 3...), whole numbers (0, 1, 2, 3...), integers (...-2, -1, 0, 1, 2...), fractions (like 1/2 for half a kilo of sugar), and decimals (like 1.5 liters of milk). Understanding how to use these numbers is a key skill for managing your money, planning your time, and even cooking delicious Rwandan meals.",
                  "examples": "Example 1:\nYour mother sends you to the market to buy 2 kilograms of Irish potatoes at 1,200 RWF per kilogram and 3 bunches of 'isambaza' (small fish) at 800 RWF per bunch.\n\nStep 1: Calculate the cost of the Irish potatoes.\nCost of potatoes = 2 kg * 1,200 RWF/kg = 2,400 RWF\n\nStep 2: Calculate the cost of the 'isambaza'.\nCost of 'isambaza' = 3 bunches * 800 RWF/bunch = 2,400 RWF\n\nStep 3: Calculate the total cost.\nTotal cost = Cost of potatoes + Cost of 'isambaza'\nTotal cost = 2,400 RWF + 2,400 RWF = 4,800 RWF\n\nTherefore, you will need 4,800 RWF to buy these items.\n\nExample 2:\nA bus travels from Kigali to Musanze. The distance is approximately 105 kilometers. If the bus travels at an average speed of 70 kilometers per hour, how long will the journey take?\n\nStep 1: Identify the formula for time.\nTime = Distance / Speed\n\nStep 2: Substitute the given values into the formula.\nTime = 105 km / 70 km/hour\n\nStep 3: Calculate the time.\nTime = 1.5 hours\n\nTherefore, the journey from Kigali to Musanze will take 1.5 hours (or 1 hour and 30 minutes).",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) You have 5,000 RWF. You spend 1,500 RWF on a book and 2,000 RWF on some fruit. How much money do you have left?\n2. (Easy) A basket contains 15 ripe bananas. If 6 of them are eaten, how many bananas are left?\n3. (Medium) A farmer plants 3 rows of maize. Each row has 20 plants. He also plants 2 rows of beans, with 15 plants in each row. How many plants did he plant in total?\n4. (Medium) You are making 'ugali' for your family. The recipe calls for 500 grams of maize flour. If you only have a 250-gram packet, how much more flour do you need?\n5. (Hard) A teacher buys 30 exercise books at 700 RWF each and 5 pens at 300 RWF each. If she pays with a 25,000 RWF note, how much change will she receive?",
                  "summary": "* Numbers are essential for daily tasks like shopping, time management, and problem-solving.\n* We use various number types: natural, whole, integers, fractions, and decimals.\n* Basic operations like addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division are used to solve problems involving numbers.\n* Real-world examples help us understand the practical use of mathematics.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "If a loaf of bread costs 700 RWF and you buy 3 loaves, how much will you spend in total?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "2,100 RWF",
                      "B": "1,400 RWF",
                      "C": "2,800 RWF",
                      "D": "703 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 1,500 RWF\n2. 9 bananas\n3. 90 plants\n4. 250 grams\n5. 2,500 RWF"
                }
              ],
