            {
              "name": "Unit 3: Percentage, Discount, Profit and Loss",
              "previous_answers": "Unit 2 Quiz Answers: 1-A, 2-B, 3-B, 4-A, 5-C, 6-A, 7-B, 8-B, 9-B, 10-A",
              "lessons": [
                "Percentage increase and decrease",
                "Discount and commission",
                "Profit and loss calculations",
                "Simple interest",
                "Real-life financial applications"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what a percentage is (out of 100)\n- Calculate a percentage of a given amount\n- Find percentage increase and decrease",
                  "reading": "A percentage is a way of saying 'out of 100'. The % sign means per hundred. If you score 80% on a test, you got 80 marks out of every 100.\n\nIn Rwanda, percentages are everywhere:\n- A shop may give a 10% discount\n- A bank might pay 5% interest on savings\n- A farmer might lose 15% of crops to weather\n\nTo find a percentage of an amount:\n- Change the % to a decimal (divide by 100)\n- Multiply by the amount\n\nExample: Find 20% of 5,000 RWF\n20% = 20 ÷ 100 = 0.20\n0.20 x 5,000 = 1,000 RWF\n\nFor percentage increase, find the increase amount and add to the original.\nFor percentage decrease, find the decrease amount and subtract from the original.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A farmer harvested 200 kg of maize. 15% was damaged by rain. How much maize was damaged?\nSolution:\n15% = 15 ÷ 100 = 0.15\nDamaged = 0.15 x 200 = 30 kg\nAnswer: 30 kg was damaged.\n\nExample 2: A shop increases the price of sugar from 1,000 RWF to 1,200 RWF. Find the percentage increase.\nSolution:\nIncrease = 1,200 – 1,000 = 200 RWF\nPercentage increase = (200 ÷ 1,000) x 100 = 20%\nAnswer: 20% increase.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What is 10% of 3,000 RWF?\n2. (Easy) Write 25% as a fraction\n3. (Medium) A shop sells a bag of rice at 12,000 RWF. The price increases by 15%. What is the new price?\n4. (Medium) A school has 400 students. 60% walk to school. How many walk?\n5. (Hard) A farmer had 1,500 cows. He sold 30% of them and then bought 100 more. How many cows does he have now?",
                  "summary": "* Percent means out of 100. 50% = 50 out of 100 = half\n* To find a % of an amount: convert % to decimal then multiply\n* For increase: find the increase amount and add to original\n* For decrease: find the decrease amount and subtract from original",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What is 15% of 4,000 RWF?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "400 RWF",
                      "B": "600 RWF",
                      "C": "800 RWF",
                      "D": "500 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 300 RWF\n2. 1/4\n3. 13,800 RWF\n4. 240 students\n5. 1,150 cows"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate a discount on an item\n- Find the sale price after a discount\n- Understand what commission is",
                  "reading": "A discount is a reduction in price. Shops in Rwanda often give discounts, especially during holidays or for bulk buying. If a shop gives 10% off, you pay 10% less.\n\nTo find the sale price:\n1. Find the discount amount (percentage x original price)\n2. Subtract discount from original price\n\nCommission is money paid to a salesperson based on what they sell. For example, a phone seller in Kigali might earn 5% commission on every phone they sell. If they sell a phone for 100,000 RWF, they earn 5% of that = 5,000 RWF.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A jacket costs 25,000 RWF. There is a 20% discount. What is the sale price?\nSolution:\nDiscount = 20% of 25,000 = (20/100) x 25,000 = 5,000 RWF\nSale price = 25,000 – 5,000 = 20,000 RWF\nAnswer: 20,000 RWF\n\nExample 2: A seller gets 8% commission on goods she sells. She sells goods worth 350,000 RWF. How much commission does she earn?\nSolution:\nCommission = 8% of 350,000 = (8/100) x 350,000 = 28,000 RWF\nAnswer: She earns 28,000 RWF",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) A 20,000 RWF TV has a 10% discount. How much do you save?\n2. (Easy) A book costs 6,000 RWF after a 25% discount. What was the original price?\n3. (Medium) A salesperson earns 12% commission. She sells goods worth 500,000 RWF. What is her commission?\n4. (Medium) A shop offers 'buy 2 get 1 free' on pens at 500 RWF each. What is the percentage discount per pen?\n5. (Hard) A laptop costs 450,000 RWF. The shop gives a 15% discount for cash payment. How much do you pay in cash?",
                  "summary": "* Discount is the amount saved. Sale price = original price – discount\n* Commission is a percentage of sales paid to a seller\n* To find the discount or commission, multiply the percentage by the original amount\n* Always check if your final price or commission makes sense",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A bag costs 15,000 RWF with a 10% discount. What is the discount amount?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "1,000 RWF",
                      "B": "1,500 RWF",
                      "C": "2,000 RWF",
                      "D": "500 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 2,000 RWF\n2. 8,000 RWF\n3. 60,000 RWF\n4. 33.3%\n5. 382,500 RWF"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate profit and loss\n- Find percentage profit and percentage loss\n- Understand cost price and selling price",
                  "reading": "In business, knowing if you made a profit or loss is very important. It's simple:\n- Profit = Selling Price – Cost Price (when you sell for more than you paid)\n- Loss = Cost Price – Selling Price (when you sell for less than you paid)\n\nExample: A farmer buys a goat for 30,000 RWF and sells it for 45,000 RWF.\nProfit = 45,000 – 30,000 = 15,000 RWF\n\nPercentage profit or loss is always based on the cost price:\n- % Profit = (Profit ÷ Cost Price) x 100\n- % Loss = (Loss ÷ Cost Price) x 100\n\nIn the example above: % Profit = (15,000 ÷ 30,000) x 100 = 50%",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A trader buys 50 kg of rice at 800 RWF per kg. He sells all of it at 1,000 RWF per kg. Find his profit and percentage profit.\nSolution:\nCost Price = 50 x 800 = 40,000 RWF\nSelling Price = 50 x 1,000 = 50,000 RWF\nProfit = 50,000 – 40,000 = 10,000 RWF\n% Profit = (10,000 ÷ 40,000) x 100 = 25%\n\nExample 2: A shopkeeper buys a shirt for 8,000 RWF and sells it for 6,500 RWF. Find the loss and percentage loss.\nSolution:\nLoss = 8,000 – 6,500 = 1,500 RWF\n% Loss = (1,500 ÷ 8,000) x 100 = 18.75%",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Cost = 5,000 RWF, Selling Price = 7,000 RWF. Profit or loss? How much?\n2. (Easy) Cost = 12,000 RWF, Selling Price = 9,000 RWF. Find the loss.\n3. (Medium) A farmer buys a cow for 150,000 RWF and sells it for 180,000 RWF. Find percentage profit.\n4. (Medium) A shopkeeper buys 100 eggs at 150 RWF each. 10 eggs break. He sells the rest at 200 RWF each. Find profit or loss.\n5. (Hard) A trader buys goods for 2,000,000 RWF and wants a 30% profit. What should the selling price be?",
                  "summary": "* Profit = Selling Price – Cost Price (sell for more than you paid)\n* Loss = Cost Price – Selling Price (sell for less than you paid)\n* % Profit or Loss = (Profit or Loss ÷ Cost Price) x 100\n* These calculations help businesses know if they are doing well",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Cost = 20,000 RWF, Selling Price = 25,000 RWF. What is the percentage profit?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "20%",
                      "B": "25%",
                      "C": "30%",
                      "D": "15%"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Profit 2,000 RWF\n2. 3,000 RWF\n3. 20%\n4. Profit 2,000 RWF\n5. 2,600,000 RWF"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what simple interest is\n- Calculate simple interest using the formula\n- Find total amount after interest",
                  "reading": "When you save money in a bank or borrow money, interest is involved. Simple interest is money you earn on savings or pay on a loan.\n\nThe formula is:\nSimple Interest (I) = Principal (P) x Rate (R) x Time (T) ÷ 100\n\nWhere:\n- Principal (P) = the amount you save or borrow\n- Rate (R) = the percentage rate per year\n- Time (T) = the number of years\nTotal Amount = Principal + Interest\n\nExample: You save 100,000 RWF in a bank at 5% per year for 3 years.\nInterest = (100,000 x 5 x 3) ÷ 100 = 15,000 RWF\nTotal = 100,000 + 15,000 = 115,000 RWF",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A farmer borrows 200,000 RWF from a bank at 8% per year for 2 years. Find the simple interest and total to repay.\nSolution:\nInterest = (200,000 x 8 x 2) ÷ 100 = 32,000 RWF\nTotal = 200,000 + 32,000 = 232,000 RWF\nAnswer: Interest = 32,000 RWF, Total = 232,000 RWF\n\nExample 2: A student saves 50,000 RWF in a savings account at 4% per year. How much interest does she earn in 5 years?\nSolution:\nInterest = (50,000 x 4 x 5) ÷ 100 = 10,000 RWF\nAnswer: She earns 10,000 RWF in interest.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find the simple interest on 100,000 RWF at 5% for 1 year.\n2. (Easy) Principal = 200,000 RWF, Rate = 10%, Time = 2 years. Find interest.\n3. (Medium) A business owner borrows 500,000 RWF at 12% per year for 3 years. Find total amount to repay.\n4. (Medium) How much interest do you earn on 80,000 RWF saved at 6% for 4 years?\n5. (Hard) A farmer wants to have 150,000 RWF after 2 years. The bank pays 5% simple interest. How much must she deposit now? (Hint: Use I = PRT/100 and Total = P + I)",
                  "summary": "* Simple interest = (Principal x Rate x Time) ÷ 100\n* Total amount = Principal + Interest\n* Principal is the original amount saved or borrowed\n* Rate is the percentage per year, Time is in years",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Simple interest on 100,000 RWF at 10% for 3 years is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "10,000 RWF",
                      "B": "20,000 RWF",
                      "C": "30,000 RWF",
                      "D": "40,000 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 5,000 RWF\n2. 40,000 RWF\n3. 680,000 RWF\n4. 19,200 RWF\n5. ~136,364 RWF"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply percentages, discounts, profit/loss and interest to real-life situations\n- Make smart financial decisions using math\n- Solve multi-step money problems",
                  "reading": "In real life, financial math problems often combine different topics. For example, a shopkeeper might get a discount from the supplier, then sell to customers at a profit. Understanding all these concepts helps you make wise decisions with money.\n\nHere are some real situations in Rwanda:\n- A farmer selling produce at Kimironko market needs to know cost price, selling price, and profit\n- A student saving pocket money needs to understand interest rates\n- A shopper looking for the best deal compares discounts\n\nWhen solving multi-step problems, work slowly step by step and always check your answer.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A shopkeeper buys a radio for 30,000 RWF. He spends 2,000 RWF on transport. He wants a 20% profit on his total cost. What should the selling price be?\nSolution:\nTotal cost = 30,000 + 2,000 = 32,000 RWF\nProfit = 20% of 32,000 = (20/100) x 32,000 = 6,400 RWF\nSelling price = 32,000 + 6,400 = 38,400 RWF\nAnswer: 38,400 RWF\n\nExample 2: A customer has 50,000 RWF. She buys a dress for 18,000 RWF (after 10% discount) and a pair of shoes for 22,000 RWF. How much does she have left?\nSolution:\nDress original price: Let original = x, x – 10% of x = 18,000, so 0.9x = 18,000, x = 20,000 RWF\nTotal spent = 18,000 + 22,000 = 40,000 RWF\nMoney left = 50,000 – 40,000 = 10,000 RWF\nAnswer: 10,000 RWF left",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) You have 10,000 RWF. You buy lunch for 3,500 RWF and a book for 4,000 RWF. How much is left?\n2. (Easy) A shirt costs 12,000 RWF. There is a 15% discount. How much do you pay?\n3. (Medium) A trader buys 100 kg of potatoes at 500 RWF per kg. He sells at 650 RWF per kg. Find total profit and % profit.\n4. (Medium) You save 60,000 RWF in a bank at 8% per year for 2 years. How much total will you have?\n5. (Hard) A family budgets 35% of their income for food, 25% for school fees, 20% for transport and saves the rest. If their monthly income is 400,000 RWF, how much do they save per month?",
                  "summary": "* Real-life money problems often combine percentages, discounts, profit and interest\n* Work step by step, reading each part carefully\n* Check if your answer makes sense in the real world\n* Financial math helps you make better money decisions in everyday life",
                  "quiz": {
