                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what direct proportion means\n- Identify when two quantities are in direct proportion\n- Solve problems using direct proportion",
                  "reading": "Two quantities are in direct proportion when they increase or decrease together at the same rate. If one doubles, the other doubles. If one halves, the other halves.\n\nFor example: If 1 kg of potatoes costs 500 RWF, then 2 kg costs 1,000 RWF, and 3 kg costs 1,500 RWF. As the weight increases, the cost increases by the same factor.\n\nWe write: y = kx (where k is the constant of proportionality)\n\nIn the potato example: Cost = 500 x Weight. Here k = 500.\n\nReal-life direct proportion in Rwanda:\n- More kilograms of rice = more money to pay\n- More hours worked = more wages earned\n- More litres of petrol = more distance travelled",
                  "examples": "Example 1: If 5 metres of fabric costs 12,500 RWF, how much does 8 metres cost?\nSolution:\nFind the cost of 1 metre: 12,500 ÷ 5 = 2,500 RWF per metre\nCost of 8 metres = 8 x 2,500 = 20,000 RWF\nAnswer: 20,000 RWF\n\nExample 2: A car uses 15 litres of fuel to travel 180 km. How far can it travel on 25 litres?\nSolution:\nPer litre: 180 ÷ 15 = 12 km per litre\nDistance for 25 litres = 25 x 12 = 300 km\nAnswer: 300 km",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) 2 kg of onions cost 1,600 RWF. How much does 1 kg cost?\n2. (Easy) 3 metres of cloth cost 9,000 RWF. How much do 5 metres cost?\n3. (Medium) A worker earns 15,000 RWF for 5 hours. How much does she earn for 8 hours?\n4. (Medium) If 6 mangoes cost 1,200 RWF, how many can you buy for 3,000 RWF?\n5. (Hard) A car travels 240 km on 20 litres of fuel. How much fuel is needed for 360 km?",
                  "summary": "* Direct proportion: as one goes up, the other goes up at the same rate\n* Find the value of 1 unit first, then multiply\n* Formula: y = kx where k is the constant of proportionality\n* Examples: cost and quantity, distance and fuel",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "If 4 pens cost 2,000 RWF, how much do 7 pens cost?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "2,500 RWF",
                      "B": "3,000 RWF",
                      "C": "3,500 RWF",
                      "D": "4,000 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 800 RWF\n2. 15,000 RWF\n3. 24,000 RWF\n4. 15 mangoes\n5. 30 litres"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what inverse proportion means\n- Identify when two quantities are in inverse proportion\n- Solve inverse proportion problems",
                  "reading": "Inverse proportion is when one quantity increases while the other decreases at the same rate. If one doubles, the other halves.\n\nFor example: If 2 workers build a wall in 6 days, then 4 workers build it in 3 days (half the time). More workers = less time.\n\nWe write: y = k/x (where k is the constant)\n\nReal-life inverse proportion in Rwanda:\n- More workers on a farm = less time to harvest\n- Faster speed = less time to reach Kigali\n- More students sharing a cost = less each person pays",
                  "examples": "Example 1: 3 workers can dig a trench in 8 days. How long will 6 workers take?\nSolution:\nMore workers = less time (inverse proportion)\n3 workers → 8 days\n1 worker → 3 x 8 = 24 days\n6 workers → 24 ÷ 6 = 4 days\nAnswer: 4 days\n\nExample 2: A car travelling at 60 km/h takes 4 hours to reach Musanze. How long at 80 km/h?\nSolution:\nFaster speed = less time\nDistance = 60 x 4 = 240 km\nTime at 80 km/h = 240 ÷ 80 = 3 hours\nAnswer: 3 hours",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) 2 people can paint a room in 6 hours. How long for 4 people?\n2. (Easy) A tap fills a tank in 10 minutes. Two taps fill in how many minutes?\n3. (Medium) 5 workers harvest a field in 12 days. How many workers are needed to finish in 4 days?\n4. (Medium) At 40 km/h, a bus journey takes 3 hours. How long at 60 km/h?\n5. (Hard) 8 students share the cost of a gift equally, each paying 3,000 RWF. If 12 students share, how much does each pay?",
                  "summary": "* Inverse proportion: as one goes up, the other goes down\n* More workers = less time. Faster speed = less time.\n* Find the total work first, then divide by the new number\n* Formula: xy = k (constant)",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "4 workers build a house in 30 days. How long for 6 workers?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "15 days",
                      "B": "20 days",
                      "C": "25 days",
                      "D": "45 days"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 3 hours\n2. 5 minutes\n3. 15 workers\n4. 2 hours\n5. 2,000 RWF"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Divide a quantity in a given ratio\n- Understand what a ratio compares\n- Write ratios in simplest form",
                  "reading": "A ratio compares two or more quantities. It tells us how much of one thing there is compared to another. For example, if a class has 20 boys and 15 girls, the ratio of boys to girls is 20:15, which simplifies to 4:3.\n\nTo divide a quantity in a given ratio:\n1. Add the parts of the ratio\n2. Divide the total by the sum to find one part\n3. Multiply each part by the value of one part\n\nExample: Share 40,000 RWF between two children in the ratio 3:2\nSum of parts = 3 + 2 = 5\nOne part = 40,000 ÷ 5 = 8,000 RWF\nChild 1: 3 x 8,000 = 24,000 RWF\nChild 2: 2 x 8,000 = 16,000 RWF\nCheck: 24,000 + 16,000 = 40,000 ✓",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A farmer has cows and goats in the ratio 5:3. He has 40 cows. How many goats does he have?\nSolution:\nCows : Goats = 5 : 3\n5 parts = 40 cows, so 1 part = 40 ÷ 5 = 8\nGoats = 3 x 8 = 24\nAnswer: 24 goats\n\nExample 2: Share 100,000 RWF among three people in the ratio 2:3:5\nSolution:\nSum = 2 + 3 + 5 = 10\nOne part = 100,000 ÷ 10 = 10,000 RWF\nPerson 1: 2 x 10,000 = 20,000 RWF\nPerson 2: 3 x 10,000 = 30,000 RWF\nPerson 3: 5 x 10,000 = 50,000 RWF\nCheck: 20,000 + 30,000 + 50,000 = 100,000 ✓",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Simplify the ratio 10:15\n2. (Easy) Divide 30,000 RWF in the ratio 2:1\n3. (Medium) The ratio of boys to girls is 4:5. There are 36 boys. Find the number of girls and total students.\n4. (Medium) Share 150,000 RWF among three siblings in the ratio 2:3:5\n5. (Hard) A contractor mixes cement and sand in the ratio 1:4. He needs 25 kg of mixture. How much cement and how much sand does he need?",
                  "summary": "* A ratio compares quantities. 3:2 means 3 parts to 2 parts\n* To share in a ratio, add the parts, find one part, then multiply\n* Ratios can be simplified by dividing by a common factor\n* Always check: the sum of the parts should equal the total",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Share 50,000 RWF in the ratio 3:2. What is the smaller share?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "15,000 RWF",
                      "B": "20,000 RWF",
                      "C": "25,000 RWF",
                      "D": "30,000 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 2:3\n2. 20,000 RWF and 10,000 RWF\n3. 45 girls, 81 total\n4. 30,000, 45,000, 75,000 RWF\n5. 5 kg cement, 20 kg sand"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Read and use scale drawings\n- Calculate actual distances from a map\n- Draw simple objects to scale",
                  "reading": "Scale drawings are pictures that are larger or smaller than the real thing. They are very useful! Architects in Kigali use scale drawings to design buildings. Map makers use scale to show large areas on a small piece of paper.\n\nA scale is written as a ratio, like 1:100 or 1:100,000.\n- 1:100 means 1 cm on the drawing = 100 cm (1 m) in real life\n- 1:100,000 means 1 cm on the map = 100,000 cm (1 km) in real life\n\nTo find actual distance from a map:\nActual distance = Map distance x Scale factor\n\nExample: On a map with scale 1:50,000, two towns are 8 cm apart. What is the actual distance?\nActual = 8 x 50,000 = 400,000 cm = 4 km",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A classroom is 12 m long. On a scale drawing with scale 1:100, how long is the classroom on paper?\nSolution:\nScale 1:100 means 1 cm on paper = 100 cm real = 1 m\nSo 12 m = 12 cm on paper\nAnswer: 12 cm\n\nExample 2: On a map with scale 1:100,000, two villages are 5 cm apart. What is the real distance in km?\nSolution:\nReal distance = 5 x 100,000 = 500,000 cm\n500,000 ÷ 100,000 = 5 km\nAnswer: 5 km (notice: 1 cm on map = 1 km when scale is 1:100,000)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Scale 1:100. A room is 8 m long. How long on paper in cm?\n2. (Easy) On a map, 1 cm = 2 km. What distance is 6 cm on the map?\n3. (Medium) Scale 1:50,000. Two towns are 12 cm apart on a map. Find actual distance in km.\n4. (Medium) A rectangular field is 200 m by 150 m. Draw it to scale 1:5,000. What are the dimensions on paper?\n5. (Hard) The distance from Kigali to Musanze is about 105 km. On a map with scale 1:500,000, what is the distance in cm?",
                  "summary": "* Scale shows the ratio between a drawing and real life\n* 1:100 means 1 unit on paper = 100 units in real life\n* To find real distance: multiply map distance by scale factor\n* Scale drawings are used in construction, maps and design",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Scale 1:200. A 6 cm line on the drawing represents how many metres in real life?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "6 m",
                      "B": "8 m",
                      "C": "10 m",
                      "D": "12 m"
                    },
                    "answer": "D"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 8 cm\n2. 12 km\n3. 6 km\n4. 4 cm x 3 cm\n5. 21 cm"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Use ratio and proportion to solve everyday problems\n- Apply these concepts in cooking, building, and business\n- Combine ratio, proportion and scale in real situations",
                  "reading": "Ratios and proportions are used all the time in Rwanda:\n\nCooking: If a recipe for 4 people uses 2 cups of rice, how much for 6 people? This is direct proportion.\n\nBuilding: A construction mix of cement and sand uses ratio 1:3. If you have 2 bags of cement, how much sand?\n\nBusiness: Sharing profits among partners according to their investment ratio.\n\nMaps: Finding real distances between places in Rwanda.\n\nA proportion states that two ratios are equal. For example, if 2:3 = 4:6, then 2/3 = 4/6 = 0.667.\n\nTo check if two ratios form a proportion, cross-multiply. If the products are equal, they are proportional.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A recipe for 'posho' (maize porridge) uses 3 cups of water for every 2 cups of maize flour. How much water is needed for 6 cups of maize flour?\nSolution:\nWater : Flour = 3 : 2\n3/2 = w/6\nCross-multiply: 3 x 6 = 2 x w, so 18 = 2w, w = 9\nAnswer: 9 cups of water\n\nExample 2: Two business partners invested 500,000 RWF and 300,000 RWF. They made a profit of 160,000 RWF. How should they share it?\nSolution:\nInvestment ratio = 500,000 : 300,000 = 5 : 3\nSum = 5 + 3 = 8\nOne part = 160,000 ÷ 8 = 20,000 RWF\nPartner 1: 5 x 20,000 = 100,000 RWF\nPartner 2: 3 x 20,000 = 60,000 RWF",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) A recipe for 4 people uses 3 eggs. How many eggs for 8 people?\n2. (Easy) If 2:5 = 6:x, find x.\n3. (Medium) Three friends invest 200,000, 300,000 and 500,000 RWF. Profit is 250,000 RWF. Share it fairly.\n4. (Medium) A map scale is 1:100,000. The distance between two schools is 7 cm on the map. What is the real distance?\n5. (Hard) A farmer has cows and goats in ratio 4:3. He buys 5 more cows and the ratio becomes 5:3. How many cows did he have originally?",
                  "summary": "* Ratio and proportion solve real problems in cooking, building, business and maps\n* A proportion says two ratios are equal. Cross-multiply to solve\n* Profit sharing uses the investment ratio\n* Always check if the answer makes sense in the real situation",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "If 2:3 = 8:x, what is x?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "10",
                      "B": "11",
                      "C": "12",
                      "D": "14"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 6 eggs\n2. x = 15\n3. 50,000, 75,000, 125,000 RWF\n4. 7 km\n5. 20 cows"
                }
              ],
