            {
              "name": "Unit 5: Points, Lines and Angles",
              "previous_answers": "Unit 4 Quiz Answers: 1-A, 2-B, 3-B, 4-C, 5-C, 6-B, 7-B, 8-C, 9-B, 10-C",
              "lessons": [
                "Points and lines",
                "Types of angles",
                "Angle relationships (complementary, supplementary)",
                "Angles formed by parallel lines",
                "Geometric constructions"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what a point and a line are\n- Name and draw points and lines\n- Identify line segments and rays",
                  "reading": "Geometry starts with the simplest ideas: points and lines.\n\nA point is a location in space. It has no size - just a position. We mark it with a dot and name it with a capital letter, like point A.\n\nA line goes on forever in both directions. We name it by any two points on it, like line AB or line l.\n\nA line segment is part of a line with two endpoints. It has a fixed length. Like a piece of string between two points.\n\nA ray has one endpoint and goes on forever in one direction. Like the light from a torch.\n\nThink about the roads in Kigali: a straight road is like a line. The distance from one junction to another is like a line segment. The light from a car's headlight is like a ray.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Draw and label:\na) Point P\nb) Line XY\nc) Line segment AB\nd) Ray CD\n\nSolution:\na) A dot labeled P\nb) A straight line with arrows at both ends, labeled XY\nc) A straight line with endpoints A and B (no arrows)\nd) A line starting at C (with a dot) going through D with an arrow at D's end\n\nExample 2: How many lines can pass through one point? Through two points?\nSolution:\n- Through one point: infinite lines can pass\n- Through two points: only one line can pass",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What is the difference between a line and a line segment?\n2. (Easy) How many endpoints does a ray have?\n3. (Medium) Draw point A, point B, and line AB.\n4. (Medium) Can three points all lie on the same line? Explain.\n5. (Hard) On a line, there are 5 points. How many line segments can you name?",
                  "summary": "* A point shows a location. A line goes on forever in both directions\n* A line segment has two endpoints. A ray has one endpoint\n* Only one line can pass through two points\n* Points, lines, segments and rays are the building blocks of geometry",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "How many endpoints does a line segment have?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0",
                      "B": "1",
                      "C": "2",
                      "D": "Infinite"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Line goes forever both ways, segment has two ends\n2. 1 endpoint\n3. (Drawing)\n4. Yes, they are collinear\n5. 10 segments"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Identify different types of angles\n- Measure and estimate angles\n- Classify angles as acute, right, obtuse or reflex",
                  "reading": "An angle is formed when two lines meet at a point. The point where they meet is called the vertex. We measure angles in degrees (°).\n\nAngles are everywhere in Rwanda:\n- The corner of a book or a classroom door is a right angle (90°)\n- The roof of a traditional house makes an acute angle (less than 90°)\n- The open position of a door can make an obtuse angle (more than 90°)\n\nTypes of angles:\n- Acute angle: less than 90° (like a sharp pencil tip)\n- Right angle: exactly 90° (like the letter L)\n- Obtuse angle: between 90° and 180° (like a wide open door)\n- Straight angle: exactly 180° (a straight line)\n- Reflex angle: more than 180° but less than 360°",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Classify these angles: 35°, 90°, 150°, 270°\nSolution:\n35° = Acute (less than 90°)\n90° = Right angle\n150° = Obtuse (between 90° and 180°)\n270° = Reflex (more than 180°)\n\nExample 2: Estimate the angle of a sloped roof on a house in Rwanda.\nSolution:\nA typical roof slope is about 30° to 45°, which is an acute angle. This allows rain to run off easily.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What type of angle is 45°?\n2. (Easy) How many degrees in a right angle?\n3. (Medium) Draw an acute angle, a right angle, and an obtuse angle.\n4. (Medium) A clock shows 3:00. What angle do the hands make?\n5. (Hard) If an angle is 15° more than a right angle, what type is it and how many degrees?",
                  "summary": "* An angle is made where two lines meet. Measured in degrees (°)\n* Acute < 90°, Right = 90°, Obtuse > 90° but < 180°\n* Straight = 180°, Reflex > 180°\n* Angles are everywhere - in buildings, furniture, clocks and nature",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What type of angle is 120°?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Acute",
                      "B": "Right",
                      "C": "Obtuse",
                      "D": "Reflex"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Acute\n2. 90°\n3. (Drawing)\n4. 90°\n5. Obtuse, 105°"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find complementary and supplementary angles\n- Understand angle relationships\n- Use angle relationships to find missing angles",
                  "reading": "Angles have special relationships. Two important ones are:\n\nComplementary angles: two angles that add up to 90°. If one is 30°, the other is 60°. Think of them as making a right angle together.\n\nSupplementary angles: two angles that add up to 180°. If one is 110°, the other is 70°. Think of them as making a straight line together.\n\nReal-life examples:\n- In Rwanda, when two walls meet at a corner of a room, they form a right angle (90°). If you put two acute angles together that add to 90°, they are complementary.\n- A straight road is 180°. Two turns that add to 180° are supplementary.\n\nComplement: 90° - given angle\nSupplement: 180° - given angle",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Find the complement of 35°.\nSolution:\nComplement = 90° - 35° = 55°\nCheck: 35° + 55° = 90° ✓\n\nExample 2: Find the supplement of 110°.\nSolution:\nSupplement = 180° - 110° = 70°\nCheck: 110° + 70° = 180° ✓\n\nExample 3: Two angles are complementary. One is 15° more than the other. Find both.\nSolution:\nLet the smaller angle = x, larger = x + 15\nx + (x + 15) = 90\n2x + 15 = 90\n2x = 75\nx = 37.5°\nAngles: 37.5° and 52.5°",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find the complement of 50°\n2. (Easy) Find the supplement of 75°\n3. (Medium) Two angles are supplementary. One is 30° more than the other. Find both.\n4. (Medium) An angle is its own complement. What is the angle?\n5. (Hard) Two angles are complementary. The larger is 3 times the smaller. Find both angles.",
                  "summary": "* Complementary angles add to 90°\n* Supplementary angles add to 180°\n* To find complement: 90° – given angle\n* To find supplement: 180° – given angle\n* These relationships help find missing angles in geometry problems",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What is the complement of 28°?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "62°",
                      "B": "72°",
                      "C": "152°",
                      "D": "28°"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 40°\n2. 105°\n3. 75° and 105°\n4. 45°\n5. 22.5° and 67.5°"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Identify parallel and perpendicular lines\n- Find angles formed when a transversal cuts parallel lines\n- Use corresponding, alternate and co-interior angles",
                  "reading": "Parallel lines are lines that never meet. They stay the same distance apart forever. In Rwanda, the railway tracks are parallel. The rows of crops on a terrace farm are also parallel.\n\nWhen a line crosses two parallel lines, it's called a transversal. This creates special angle relationships:\n\n- Corresponding angles: equal (same position at each intersection)\n- Alternate angles: equal (inside the parallel lines, on opposite sides of the transversal)\n- Co-interior angles: add to 180° (inside the parallel lines, on the same side of the transversal)\n\nIf you know one angle, you can find all the others using these rules!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: In a diagram of parallel lines cut by a transversal, one angle (on the top left) is 70°. Find the other angles.\nSolution:\n- Top right = 110° (supplementary to 70°)\n- Bottom left = 70° (corresponding to top left)\n- Bottom right = 110° (corresponding to top right)\n- The alternate angles are also equal\n\nExample 2: Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One co-interior angle is 65°. Find the other.\nSolution:\nCo-interior angles add to 180°\nOther angle = 180° - 65° = 115°",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What does 'parallel' mean?\n2. (Easy) If one corresponding angle is 55°, what is the other?\n3. (Medium) Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle is 120°. Find the adjacent angle.\n4. (Medium) Co-interior angles add to how many degrees?\n5. (Hard) In a diagram with parallel lines, one alternate angle is (3x + 10)° and its pair is 70°. Find x.",
                  "summary": "* Parallel lines never meet. A transversal crosses them\n* Corresponding angles are equal\n* Alternate angles are equal\n* Co-interior angles add to 180°\n* These rules let you find all angles if you know just one",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "When parallel lines are cut by a transversal, what is true about alternate angles?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "They add to 180°",
                      "B": "They are equal",
                      "C": "They add to 90°",
                      "D": "They are different"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Lines that never meet\n2. 55°\n3. 60°\n4. 180°\n5. x = 20"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Use a ruler and protractor to draw geometric shapes\n- Construct angles and lines accurately\n- Draw perpendicular and parallel lines",
                  "reading": "Geometric constructions are precise drawings made using only a ruler (or straightedge) and a compass. No measuring! This is how architects and engineers create accurate plans.\n\nIn Rwanda, builders use geometric constructions to make sure walls are straight, corners are right angles, and roofs are properly sloped.\n\nBasic constructions:\n- Draw a line segment of a given length\n- Construct a perpendicular line (at 90°)\n- Construct a parallel line\n- Bisect an angle (cut it in half)\n- Construct angles like 60° and 90° without a protractor\n\nFor accurate constructions, always keep your compass sharp and your pencil fine.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Construct a perpendicular line through a point on a line.\nSolution:\n1. Draw a line and mark point P on it\n2. Open the compass and draw arcs on both sides of P\n3. Widen the compass. From each arc, draw arcs that cross above P\n4. Draw a line through P and the crossing point - this is perpendicular\n\nExample 2: Bisect a 60° angle.\nSolution:\n1. Draw a 60° angle using a compass\n2. Place compass at vertex, draw an arc crossing both sides\n3. From where the arc meets each side, draw two arcs that cross\n4. Draw a line from vertex to the crossing point - this bisects the angle into two 30° angles",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What tools do you need for geometric constructions?\n2. (Easy) What does 'bisect' mean?\n3. (Medium) Construct a 90° angle using a ruler and compass.\n4. (Medium) Draw a line segment of 6 cm, then construct a perpendicular line at its midpoint.\n5. (Hard) Construct an equilateral triangle with sides 5 cm using only a ruler and compass.",
                  "summary": "* Geometric constructions use only a ruler and compass, no measuring\n* You can draw perpendicular lines, parallel lines, and bisect angles\n* Constructions are used in building, architecture and design\n* Accuracy comes from keeping tools steady and marks clear",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What does it mean to bisect an angle?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Double it",
                      "B": "Cut it in half",
                      "C": "Make it 90°",
                      "D": "Draw it parallel"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Ruler and compass\n2. Cut in half\n3-5: (Constructions)"
                }
              ],
