            {
              "name": "Unit 6: Solids",
              "previous_answers": "Unit 5 Quiz Answers: 1-B, 2-B, 3-B, 4-D, 5-A, 6-A, 7-B, 8-A, 9-B, 10-B",
              "lessons": [
                "Properties of 3D shapes",
                "Volume of prisms, cylinders and spheres",
                "Surface area of solids",
                "Nets of solids",
                "Real-life applications of volume and surface area"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Identify common 3D shapes\n- Describe their properties (faces, edges, vertices)\n- Classify solids by their features",
                  "reading": "Solids are 3D (three-dimensional) shapes. They have length, width and height. Unlike flat 2D shapes, solids take up space.\n\nEvery solid has:\n- Faces: the flat surfaces (like the sides of a box)\n- Edges: where two faces meet (like the corner of a table)\n- Vertices: corners where edges meet (singular: vertex)\n\nCommon 3D shapes in Rwanda:\n- Cube: like a dice or a sugar cube\n- Cuboid: like a brick or a classroom\n- Cylinder: like a drum for water or a can of milk\n- Sphere: like a football or an orange\n- Cone: like a traditional hat or an ice cream cone\n- Pyramid: like the roof of a house or the top of a mountain",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A cube has how many faces, edges and vertices?\nSolution:\n- Faces: 6 (like a dice)\n- Edges: 12\n- Vertices: 8\n\nExample 2: Compare a cylinder and a cuboid.\nSolution:\n- Cylinder: 2 flat circular faces, 1 curved face, no vertices, 2 edges\n- Cuboid: 6 rectangular faces, 12 edges, 8 vertices",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) How many faces does a cube have?\n2. (Easy) What shape is a football?\n3. (Medium) Draw a table showing the number of faces, edges and vertices for: cube, cuboid, cylinder, sphere, cone.\n4. (Medium) A triangular prism has how many faces?\n5. (Hard) A square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges. How many vertices does it have? (Hint: Euler's formula: F + V = E + 2)",
                  "summary": "* 3D solids have faces (flat surfaces), edges (where faces meet), and vertices (corners)\n* Common shapes: cube, cuboid, cylinder, sphere, cone, pyramid\n* Each shape has a specific number of faces, edges and vertices\n* Understanding these properties is the first step in geometry of solids",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "How many vertices does a cube have?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "6",
                      "B": "8",
                      "C": "10",
                      "D": "12"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 6\n2. Sphere\n3. (Table)\n4. 5\n5. 5 vertices"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what volume means\n- Calculate volume of prisms, cylinders and spheres\n- Use the correct formula for each shape",
                  "reading": "Volume is the amount of space a 3D shape takes up. Think of it like how much water a tank can hold. Volume is measured in cubic units (cm³, m³).\n\nFormulas:\n- Cuboid (box): Volume = length x width x height\n- Cube: Volume = side x side x side = side³\n- Cylinder: Volume = π x r² x height (where r = radius)\n- Sphere: Volume = 4/3 x π x r³\n- Prism: Volume = area of base x height\n\nReal examples in Rwanda:\n- A water tank at a school in Kigali is cylindrical. Knowing its volume tells you how much water it holds\n- A farmer storing maize in a cuboid container needs to know the volume",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A cuboid water tank is 2 m long, 1.5 m wide and 1 m high. Find the volume.\nSolution:\nVolume = 2 x 1.5 x 1 = 3 m³\nSince 1 m³ = 1,000 litres, the tank holds 3,000 litres.\n\nExample 2: A cylindrical drum has radius 0.4 m and height 1.2 m. Find its volume. (π = 3.14)\nSolution:\nVolume = π x r² x h = 3.14 x (0.4)² x 1.2\n= 3.14 x 0.16 x 1.2\n= 3.14 x 0.192 = 0.603 m³ (about 603 litres)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) A cube has side 3 cm. Find its volume.\n2. (Easy) A box is 5 cm by 4 cm by 2 cm. Find the volume.\n3. (Medium) A cylindrical water tank has radius 1 m and height 2 m. Find volume. (π = 3.14)\n4. (Medium) A sphere has radius 7 cm. Find its volume. (π = 22/7)\n5. (Hard) A rectangular prism has length 10 cm, width 6 cm, and volume 300 cm³. Find its height.",
                  "summary": "* Volume = space a solid takes up, measured in cubic units\n* Cuboid: length x width x height\n* Cylinder: πr²h\n* Sphere: 4/3 πr³\n* 1 m³ = 1,000 litres for water",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A cube has side 5 cm. Its volume is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "25 cm³",
                      "B": "100 cm³",
                      "C": "125 cm³",
                      "D": "150 cm³"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 27 cm³\n2. 40 cm³\n3. 6.28 m³\n4. 1,437.3 cm³\n5. 5 cm"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what surface area is\n- Calculate the surface area of cubes, cuboids and cylinders\n- Find surface area of spheres",
                  "reading": "Surface area is the total area of all the faces of a 3D shape. Think of it as the amount of wrapping paper needed to cover a gift box.\n\nFormulas:\n- Cube: Surface Area = 6 x side² (6 faces, each is side²)\n- Cuboid: SA = 2(lw + lh + wh) where l=length, w=width, h=height\n- Cylinder: SA = 2πr² + 2πrh (2 circles + curved face)\n- Sphere: SA = 4πr²\n\nReal-life use in Rwanda:\n- A painter needs to know how much paint to buy for a water tank\n- A carpenter making a wooden box needs to know how much wood to buy\n- A manufacturer wrapping products needs to know how much paper to use",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Find the surface area of a cube with side 4 cm.\nSolution:\nSA = 6 x (4)² = 6 x 16 = 96 cm²\n\nExample 2: A cylindrical tank has radius 2 m and height 3 m. Find its surface area. (π = 3.14)\nSolution:\nSA = 2πr² + 2πrh\n= 2 x 3.14 x 4 + 2 x 3.14 x 2 x 3\n= 25.12 + 37.68 = 62.80 m²",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find the surface area of a cube with side 2 cm.\n2. (Easy) A cuboid is 3 cm x 4 cm x 5 cm. Find its surface area.\n3. (Medium) A cylindrical can has radius 5 cm and height 12 cm. Find its surface area. (π = 3.14)\n4. (Medium) A sphere has radius 10 cm. Find its surface area. (π = 3.14)\n5. (Hard) A closed cuboid box has length 20 cm, width 15 cm and surface area 1,300 cm². Find its height.",
                  "summary": "* Surface area = total area of all faces of a 3D shape\n* Cube: 6 x side²\n* Cuboid: 2(lw + lh + wh)\n* Cylinder: 2πr² + 2πrh\n* Sphere: 4πr²",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A cube has side 3 cm. Its surface area is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "36 cm²",
                      "B": "45 cm²",
                      "C": "54 cm²",
                      "D": "60 cm²"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 24 cm²\n2. 94 cm²\n3. 534 cm²\n4. 1,256 cm²\n5. 10 cm"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what a net of a solid is\n- Draw nets for common 3D shapes\n- Match nets to their solids",
                  "reading": "A net is a flat pattern that can be folded to make a 3D shape. Think of a cardboard box that you unfold flat - that flat shape is its net.\n\nNets help us see all the faces of a solid at once. They are very useful for calculating surface area and for making models.\n\nExamples:\n- The net of a cube has 6 squares connected in a cross shape\n- The net of a cuboid has 6 rectangles\n- The net of a cylinder has 2 circles and 1 rectangle\n- The net of a cone has 1 circle and 1 sector (like a pizza slice)\n\nIn Rwanda, carpenters use nets when cutting wood to make boxes. Packaging designers use nets to create boxes for products.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Draw the net of a cube.\nSolution:\nA cube net has 6 equal squares. One way: 4 squares in a row (like a cross), with 1 square above the second and 1 below the second.\n\nExample 2: A cylinder net has which shapes?\nSolution:\nA cylinder net has:\n- 2 circles (top and bottom)\n- 1 rectangle (the curved surface, where width = circumference of circle = 2πr and height = cylinder height)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) How many squares are in the net of a cube?\n2. (Easy) What shapes make the net of a cylinder?\n3. (Medium) Draw two different nets of a cube.\n4. (Medium) Draw the net of a cuboid that is 4 cm by 3 cm by 2 cm.\n5. (Hard) A square-based pyramid has which shapes in its net? Draw it.",
                  "summary": "* A net is a flat pattern that folds into a 3D shape\n* Cube net: 6 squares. Cuboid net: 6 rectangles. Cylinder net: 2 circles + 1 rectangle\n* Nets help visualize all faces of a solid\n* Used in packaging, carpentry and design",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "How many circles are in the net of a cylinder?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "1",
                      "B": "2",
                      "C": "3",
                      "D": "0"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 6\n2. 2 circles + 1 rectangle\n3-5: (Drawings)"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply volume and surface area to real-life situations\n- Solve word problems involving solids\n- Use the right formula for the right situation",
                  "reading": "Volume and surface area are used every day in Rwanda:\n\nConstruction: A builder needs to know how many cubic metres of concrete for a foundation.\n\nWater: How much water can a tank hold? Volume tells us. How much paint to cover the tank? Surface area.\n\nFarming: How much maize fits in a storage bin? Volume. How much wood for the bin? Surface area.\n\nPackaging: How much cardboard for a box? Surface area. How many boxes fit in a truck? Volume.\n\nWhen solving:\n1. Identify the shape\n2. Choose the correct formula (volume or surface area)\n3. Substitute the numbers\n4. Check your answer makes sense",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A school water tank is a cylinder with radius 1.5 m and height 2 m. How many litres of water can it hold? (π = 3.14)\nSolution:\nVolume = πr²h = 3.14 x (1.5)² x 2\n= 3.14 x 2.25 x 2 = 14.13 m³\nLitres = 14.13 x 1,000 = 14,130 litres\n\nExample 2: A carpenter makes a cuboid wooden box, 60 cm by 40 cm by 30 cm. She wants to varnish the outside. How much area to varnish?\nSolution:\nSA = 2(lw + lh + wh)\n= 2(60x40 + 60x30 + 40x30)\n= 2(2,400 + 1,800 + 1,200)\n= 2 x 5,400 = 10,800 cm²",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) A rectangular container is 10 cm x 8 cm x 5 cm. Find its volume.\n2. (Easy) A cube-shaped box has side 12 cm. Find the cardboard needed.\n3. (Medium) A cylindrical water drum has radius 0.5 m and height 1.5 m. How many litres can it hold?\n4. (Medium) A room is 8 m long, 6 m wide and 3 m high. Find the volume of air in the room.\n5. (Hard) A farmer stores maize in a cuboid bin 2 m by 1.5 m by 1.2 m. If 1 m³ holds 700 kg of maize, how much maize can the bin store?",
                  "summary": "* Volume = space inside, used for capacity. Surface area = outside area, used for covering\n* Choose the right formula for the shape\n* 1 m³ = 1,000 litres for water\n* These calculations are used in construction, farming, packaging and everyday life",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A water tank is a cylinder with radius 1 m and height 2 m. How many litres can it hold? (π = 3.14)",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "3,140 litres",
                      "B": "6,280 litres",
                      "C": "4,710 litres",
                      "D": "5,000 litres"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 400 cm³\n2. 864 cm²\n3. 1,177.5 L\n4. 144 m³\n5. 2,520 kg"
                }
              ],
