            {
              "name": "Unit 7: Statistics",
              "previous_answers": "Unit 6 Quiz Answers: 1-C, 2-C, 3-C, 4-C, 5-C, 6-D, 7-C, 8-C, 9-B, 10-C",
              "lessons": [
                "Data collection methods",
                "Organizing data in tables",
                "Bar graphs and pictographs",
                "Mean, median and mode",
                "Interpreting statistical data"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what data is\n- Identify different ways to collect data\n- Choose the right method for collecting information",
                  "reading": "Data is information collected about people, things or events. In Rwanda, the National Institute of Statistics collects data about population, farming, schools and health. This helps the government make good decisions.\n\nWays to collect data:\n- Surveys: asking people questions (like asking students their favourite subject)\n- Observations: watching and recording (like counting cars passing a road)\n- Experiments: doing tests and recording results\n- Interviews: talking to people one-on-one\n- Existing records: using information already available (like school attendance records)\n\nData can be:\n- Primary data: collected by you for the first time\n- Secondary data: already collected by someone else",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A school wants to know what food students prefer for lunch. What method should they use?\nSolution:\nA survey or questionnaire. Ask each student or a sample of students what they prefer.\n\nExample 2: A farmer wants to know how many eggs her chickens lay each day. What method?\nSolution:\nObservation. Count and record the eggs collected every day.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What is data?\n2. (Easy) Give one method of collecting data\n3. (Medium) A researcher wants to know the most common job in a village. What method would you suggest and why?\n4. (Medium) What is the difference between primary and secondary data?\n5. (Hard) A student wants to know if more boys or girls play football at school. Design a simple data collection plan.",
                  "summary": "* Data is information collected for a purpose\n* Methods: surveys, observation, experiments, interviews, records\n* Primary data = you collect it. Secondary data = already exists\n* Good data collection leads to good decisions",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Which method would you use to find the most popular subject in your class?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Experiment",
                      "B": "Survey",
                      "C": "Observation",
                      "D": "Existing records"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Information collected\n2. Survey, observation, experiment, etc.\n3. Survey\n4. Primary = you collect, Secondary = already exists\n5. (Design plan)"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Organize data into a frequency table\n- Read and understand a frequency table\n- Group data into class intervals",
                  "reading": "After collecting data, we need to organize it so it makes sense. A frequency table is a simple way to do this.\n\nA frequency table has two columns:\n- The item or value\n- The frequency (how many times it occurs)\n\nExample: 20 students were asked their favourite fruit:\nMango, Orange, Mango, Banana, Orange, Mango, Apple, Banana, Mango, Orange, Banana, Mango, Orange, Mango, Apple, Banana, Mango, Orange, Mango, Banana\n\nFavourite Fruit | Frequency\nMango | 8\nOrange | 5\nBanana | 5\nApple | 2\nTotal | 20\n\nFor large data sets, we group data into class intervals, like 0-10, 11-20, etc.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: The heights (cm) of 10 students: 145, 150, 148, 152, 147, 150, 149, 151, 146, 150. Make a frequency table.\nSolution:\nHeight (cm) | Frequency\n145 | 1\n146 | 1\n147 | 1\n148 | 1\n149 | 1\n150 | 3\n151 | 1\n152 | 1\nTotal | 10\n\nExample 2: Group these ages into class intervals of 5 years: 7, 12, 15, 8, 22, 25, 18, 30, 9, 14, 27, 11, 19, 23, 6\nSolution:\nAge | Frequency\n6-10 | 3\n11-15 | 4\n16-20 | 2\n21-25 | 3\n26-30 | 3\nTotal | 15",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What is a frequency table used for?\n2. (Easy) In a frequency table, what does 'frequency' mean?\n3. (Medium) Here are shoe sizes: 7, 8, 7, 9, 8, 7, 10, 8, 9, 7. Make a frequency table.\n4. (Medium) Why might we group data into class intervals?\n5. (Hard) The ages of 20 people: 2, 5, 7, 12, 15, 18, 22, 25, 28, 30, 4, 8, 11, 14, 17, 20, 24, 27, 32, 35. Group into intervals 0-9, 10-19, 20-29, 30-39.",
                  "summary": "* A frequency table organizes data by counting how often each value appears\n* Frequency = the count\n* For large data sets, group into class intervals\n* Tables make it easy to see patterns and totals",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "In a frequency table of favourite colours, 'Blue' appears 7 times. What is its frequency?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "5",
                      "B": "6",
                      "C": "7",
                      "D": "8"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Organize data\n2. The count\n3. (Table)\n4. To make large data manageable\n5. (Grouped table)"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Draw a bar graph from a frequency table\n- Draw a pictograph using symbols\n- Read and interpret information from graphs",
                  "reading": "Graphs make data visual and easy to understand. Two common types are:\n\nBar graph: uses bars of different heights to show data. Each bar represents a category. The taller the bar, the bigger the number.\n\nPictograph: uses pictures or symbols to show data. Each picture stands for a certain number of items.\n\nIn Rwanda, newspapers and TV often use bar graphs to show things like crop production per district or school enrolment numbers.\n\nTo draw a bar graph:\n1. Draw a horizontal axis (categories) and vertical axis (frequency)\n2. Draw bars of equal width at the height of each frequency\n3. Label everything clearly\n\nTo draw a pictograph:\n1. Choose a symbol (like a cow for farm animals)\n2. Decide what each symbol represents (e.g., 1 cow = 10 animals)\n3. Draw the correct number of symbols for each category",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Draw a bar graph for this data: Mango = 8, Orange = 5, Banana = 5, Apple = 2\nSolution:\nVertical axis: 0 to 10 (frequency)\nHorizontal axis: Mango, Orange, Banana, Apple\nDraw bars of height 8, 5, 5, 2 with equal widths.\n\nExample 2: A pictograph showing cows on three farms. Let 1 cow symbol = 5 cows.\nFarm A: 20 cows = 4 cow symbols\nFarm B: 15 cows = 3 cow symbols\nFarm C: 25 cows = 5 cow symbols",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What does a bar graph use to show data?\n2. (Easy) In a pictograph, what does the key tell you?\n3. (Medium) Draw a bar graph for this data: Math = 12, Science = 8, English = 6, History = 4\n4. (Medium) Draw a pictograph for: Monday = 30 eggs, Tuesday = 45 eggs, Wednesday = 35 eggs (let 1 egg symbol = 5 eggs)\n5. (Hard) A school has 200 boys and 180 girls. Draw a bar graph. Also make a pictograph where 1 person = 20 students.",
                  "summary": "* Bar graphs use bar heights to show frequencies. Easy to compare categories\n* Pictographs use symbols. Each symbol represents a certain number\n* Always label axes and include a key for pictographs\n* Graphs help us see patterns quickly",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "In a bar graph showing favourite food, the tallest bar represents:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "The least popular food",
                      "B": "The most popular food",
                      "C": "The average food",
                      "D": "No food"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. Bars\n2. What each symbol represents\n3-5: (Graphs)"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate the mean (average) of a data set\n- Find the median (middle value)\n- Identify the mode (most common value)",
                  "reading": "Mean, median and mode are called 'measures of central tendency'. They tell us about the typical value in a data set.\n\nMean (average): Add all values, then divide by how many values there are.\nExample: Scores 5, 8, 10, 12, 15. Mean = (5+8+10+12+15)/5 = 50/5 = 10\n\nMedian: Arrange values in order, then find the middle value.\n- If odd number of values: median is the middle one\n- If even number: median is the average of the two middle values\n\nMode: The value that appears most often. A data set can have no mode, one mode, or multiple modes.\n\nReal example: A teacher in Rwanda calculates the mean score to know the class average. The principal uses the median salary to understand typical teacher pay.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Find the mean, median and mode of: 3, 7, 8, 5, 7, 9, 6\nSolution:\nMean = (3+7+8+5+7+9+6)/7 = 45/7 = 6.43\nOrdered: 3, 5, 6, 7, 7, 8, 9. Median = 7 (the 4th value)\nMode = 7 (appears twice)\n\nExample 2: Find the median of: 12, 15, 10, 18, 20, 14\nSolution:\nOrdered: 10, 12, 14, 15, 18, 20 (even number)\nMedian = (14+15)/2 = 14.5",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find the mean of: 4, 8, 12, 16\n2. (Easy) Find the mode of: 2, 5, 2, 7, 8, 2, 9\n3. (Medium) Find the mean, median and mode of: 10, 15, 20, 25, 20, 15, 10, 20\n4. (Medium) Five students scored: 14, 18, 20, 22, 26. Find the mean and median.\n5. (Hard) The mean of 6 numbers is 15. Five numbers are 12, 18, 14, 16, 10. Find the sixth number.",
                  "summary": "* Mean = total ÷ number of values (the average)\n* Median = middle value when ordered from smallest to largest\n* Mode = most frequent value\n* Each gives a different type of 'typical' value. Use the right one for your data",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Find the mean of: 6, 10, 14, 8, 12",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "8",
                      "B": "10",
                      "C": "12",
                      "D": "14"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 10\n2. 2\n3. Mean=16.6, Median=17.5, Mode=20\n4. Mean=20, Median=20\n5. 20"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Read information from tables and graphs\n- Draw conclusions from statistical data\n- Make simple predictions based on data",
                  "reading": "Once data is collected, organized and displayed, we need to interpret it. This means reading the information and understanding what it tells us.\n\nSteps to interpret data:\n1. Read the title and labels - what is this data about?\n2. Look at the numbers - what are the highest and lowest values?\n3. Find patterns - is something increasing or decreasing?\n4. Draw conclusions - what does this data tell us?\n5. Make decisions - what should we do based on this data?\n\nExample from Rwanda: A graph showing crop production over 5 years might show an increase. This tells us farming is improving. The government might then decide to invest more in agriculture.\n\nAlways ask: Is the data reliable? Was it collected properly? Can we trust the conclusions?",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A bar graph shows rainfall (mm) in Kigali for 6 months: Jan=80, Feb=90, Mar=120, Apr=150, May=100, Jun=40\nInterpretation:\n- Most rain in April (150 mm)\n- Least rain in June (40 mm)\n- Rain increases from Jan to Apr, then decreases\n- This is typical for Rwanda's rainy seasons\n\nExample 2: A table shows the number of students in a school over 4 years: 2019=400, 2020=420, 2021=460, 2022=500\nInterpretation:\n- School population is growing each year\n- Increase is about 20-40 students per year\n- School may need more classrooms soon",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) In a bar graph, which bar tells you the largest category?\n2. (Easy) If the mean score in a test is 15/20, is that good?\n3. (Medium) A table shows: Mon=30 eggs, Tue=35, Wed=25, Thu=40, Fri=50. Which day had the most eggs? What might explain this?\n4. (Medium) A graph shows school enrolment increasing each year. What conclusion can you draw?\n5. (Hard) Two classes took the same test. Class A mean = 16/20, Class B mean = 12/20. What can you conclude? What questions would you ask before making a decision?",
                  "summary": "* Interpreting means reading, understanding and drawing conclusions from data\n* Look for patterns: highest, lowest, increasing, decreasing\n* Use data to make decisions and predictions\n* Always check if the data is reliable before drawing conclusions",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A graph shows rainfall increasing from January to April. What can you conclude?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "It gets colder",
                      "B": "The rainy season is coming",
                      "C": "It never rains",
                      "D": "April is the driest month"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. The tallest one\n2. Depends on context\n3. Friday=50, busiest day\n4. More students each year\n5. Class A is better prepared"
                }
              ],
