            {
              "name": "Unit 8: Probability",
              "previous_answers": "Unit 7 Quiz Answers: 1-B, 2-B, 3-B, 4-C, 5-A, 6-C, 7-C, 8-C, 9-B, 10-B",
              "lessons": [
                "Basic probability concepts",
                "Sample space and events",
                "Probability of single events",
                "Experimental probability",
                "Applications of probability in daily life"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what probability means\n- Know that probability is between 0 and 1\n- Use words like certain, likely, unlikely, impossible",
                  "reading": "Probability is about the chance of something happening. We use it every day:\n- 'There is a 70% chance of rain today in Kigali'\n- 'I will probably pass my exam if I study'\n- 'It is impossible to get a 7 on a standard dice'\n\nProbability is measured from 0 to 1:\n- 0 = impossible (will never happen)\n- 1 = certain (will definitely happen)\n- 0.5 = equal chance (like a coin flip)\n\nWe can also use words:\n- Impossible: 0 chance\n- Unlikely: small chance\n- Equally likely: 50-50\n- Likely: good chance\n- Certain: 100%\n\nProbability helps us make decisions when we are not sure what will happen.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: What is the probability of the sun rising tomorrow?\nSolution:\nThe sun always rises. So probability = 1 (certain)\n\nExample 2: What is the probability of rolling a 7 on a standard dice?\nSolution:\nA standard dice has numbers 1 to 6. 7 is impossible. Probability = 0\n\nExample 3: A newborn baby is either a boy or girl. What is the probability of a girl?\nSolution:\nAbout 1/2 or 0.5 (equally likely)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) What probability means something will definitely happen?\n2. (Easy) What is the probability of a coin landing on heads?\n3. (Medium) A bag has only red marbles. What is the probability of picking a blue one?\n4. (Medium) Classify these as impossible, unlikely, equally likely, likely, or certain: a) It will rain in Rwanda this year b) You will fly like a bird c) A dice shows an even number\n5. (Hard) Give an example of an event with probability 0.25.",
                  "summary": "* Probability measures chance from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain)\n* 0.5 = equal chance. 0 = never. 1 = always\n* Words: impossible, unlikely, equally likely, likely, certain\n* Probability helps us make decisions about uncertain events",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What is the probability of a coin landing on tails?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0",
                      "B": "0.25",
                      "C": "0.5",
                      "D": "1"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 1 (certain)\n2. 0.5\n3. 0\n4. a) Certain, b) Impossible, c) Equally likely\n5. E.g., Drawing a specific card from 4 cards"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Define sample space\n- List all possible outcomes of an event\n- Understand what an event is",
                  "reading": "The sample space is the set of all possible outcomes. An event is a specific outcome or group of outcomes we are interested in.\n\nExample: When rolling a dice:\n- Sample space = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} (all possible outcomes)\n- Event 'even number' = {2, 4, 6}\n- Event 'greater than 4' = {5, 6}\n\nExample: When flipping a coin:\n- Sample space = {Heads, Tails}\n- Event 'Heads' = {Heads}\n\nIn Rwanda, we use probability when predicting rain. The sample space for weather could be {Rain, No Rain}. Each day's weather is an outcome.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: List the sample space when you spin a spinner with colours Red, Blue, Green.\nSolution:\nSample space = {Red, Blue, Green}\n\nExample 2: Two coins are tossed. List the sample space.\nSolution:\n{HH, HT, TH, TT} (H = heads, T = tails)\nEvent 'at least one head' = {HH, HT, TH}\nEvent 'two tails' = {TT}",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) When rolling a dice, what is the sample space?\n2. (Easy) If a bag has red, blue and green marbles, what is the sample space for picking one colour?\n3. (Medium) List the sample space for a coin flipped three times.\n4. (Medium) For a dice, list the event 'odd numbers'.\n5. (Hard) A spinner has numbers 1 to 8. List the event 'multiple of 3' and 'prime numbers'.",
                  "summary": "* Sample space = all possible outcomes\n* Event = the outcome(s) we are interested in\n* Listing outcomes helps us count possibilities\n* Total outcomes = number of items in the sample space",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "What is the sample space for rolling a standard dice?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}",
                      "B": "{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}",
                      "C": "{0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}",
                      "D": "{2, 4, 6}"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. {1,2,3,4,5,6}\n2. {Red, Blue, Green}\n3. {HHH,HHT,HTH,HTT,THH,THT,TTH,TTT}\n4. {1,3,5}\n5. Multiples of 3: {3,6}, Primes: {2,3,5,7}"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate probability of a single event\n- Use the formula: P(event) = favourable outcomes / total outcomes\n- Express probability as a fraction, decimal or percentage",
                  "reading": "To find the probability of a single event, use this formula:\n\nP(event) = Number of favourable outcomes ÷ Total number of possible outcomes\n\nExample: What is the probability of rolling a 4 on a dice?\n- Favourable outcomes: {4} = 1\n- Total outcomes: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6} = 6\n- P(4) = 1/6\n\nWe can express probability as:\n- Fraction: 1/6\n- Decimal: 0.167\n- Percentage: 16.7%\n\nReal example: In a class of 30 students in Rwanda, 12 are girls. The probability of picking a girl at random = 12/30 = 2/5 = 0.4 = 40%",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A bag has 5 red, 3 blue and 2 green marbles. Find P(blue).\nSolution:\nFavourable (blue) = 3\nTotal = 5 + 3 + 2 = 10\nP(blue) = 3/10 = 0.3 = 30%\n\nExample 2: A letter is chosen from the word 'R W A N D A'. Find P(vowel).\nSolution:\nLetters: R, W, A, N, D, A (6 letters)\nVowels: A, A (2 vowels)\nP(vowel) = 2/6 = 1/3 = 0.333 = 33.3%",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) A dice is rolled. Find P(5)\n2. (Easy) A coin is flipped. Find P(heads)\n3. (Medium) A bag has 4 red, 6 blue, 10 total marbles. Find P(red) and P(blue)\n4. (Medium) From the word 'KIGALI', find P(vowel) and P(consonant)\n5. (Hard) A box has 8 red balls and some blue balls. P(red) = 2/5. How many blue balls are there?",
                  "summary": "* P(event) = favourable outcomes ÷ total outcomes\n* Probability can be a fraction, decimal or percentage\n* Always count carefully: favourable and total\n* Check: probability should be between 0 and 1",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A bag has 3 red and 7 blue marbles. P(red) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "3/10",
                      "B": "7/10",
                      "C": "3/7",
                      "D": "1/3"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 1/6\n2. 1/2\n3. 2/5, 3/5\n4. 3/7 vowel, 4/7 consonant\n5. 12 blue balls"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand experimental probability\n- Conduct simple experiments to find probability\n- Compare theoretical and experimental probability",
                  "reading": "Theoretical probability is what we expect to happen. Experimental probability is what actually happens when we do an experiment.\n\nFor example, if you flip a coin 10 times, you expect 5 heads (theoretical probability = 0.5). But you might get 6 heads and 4 tails. That's experimental probability.\n\nExperimental Probability = Number of times event occurs ÷ Total number of trials\n\nThe more trials you do, the closer experimental probability gets to theoretical probability. This is called the Law of Large Numbers.\n\nExample: A farmer in Rwanda records the weather for 30 days: 18 sunny, 12 rainy.\nExperimental P(sunny) = 18/30 = 0.6\nExperimental P(rainy) = 12/30 = 0.4",
                  "examples": "Example 1: A student rolled a dice 60 times. She got a '6' eight times. Experimental P(6) = ?\nSolution:\nExperimental P(6) = 8/60 = 2/15 = 0.133\nTheoretical P(6) = 1/6 = 0.167\nThe experimental value (0.133) is close to theoretical (0.167).\n\nExample 2: A shopkeeper recorded 120 customers: 45 bought something, 75 just looked.\nExperimental P(buy) = 45/120 = 3/8 = 0.375 = 37.5%\nThis is useful: the shopkeeper knows about 38% of customers will buy.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) You flip a coin 20 times and get 12 heads. What is experimental P(heads)?\n2. (Easy) A dice is rolled 30 times. A '3' appears 4 times. Experimental P(3) = ?\n3. (Medium) A student did a 50-question test and got 40 correct. What is experimental P(correct)?\n4. (Medium) Why does experimental probability get closer to theoretical with more trials?\n5. (Hard) A spinner has 4 colours: Red, Blue, Green, Yellow. After 100 spins, the results are: Red=20, Blue=35, Green=25, Yellow=20. Do you think the spinner is fair? Explain.",
                  "summary": "* Experimental probability = what actually happens in an experiment\n* Formula: number of times event occurs ÷ total trials\n* More trials = more accurate (closer to theoretical)\n* Experimental probability is useful for real-world predictions",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A coin is flipped 50 times and gets 28 heads. Experimental P(heads) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.5",
                      "B": "0.56",
                      "C": "0.44",
                      "D": "0.28"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. 12/20 = 0.6\n2. 4/30 ≈ 0.133\n3. 40/50 = 0.8\n4. More trials = more accurate\n5. Not fair, Blue appears too often"
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply probability to real-life situations\n- Make decisions based on probability\n- Understand how probability is used in Rwanda",
                  "reading": "Probability is used everywhere in real life:\n\nWeather: The Rwanda Meteorology Agency uses probability to predict rain. '80% chance of rain' means there is a high likelihood.\n\nAgriculture: A farmer might say 'there is a 70% chance my crops will do well this season' based on past years.\n\nHealth: Doctors talk about the probability of a treatment working.\n\nBusiness: An entrepreneur calculates the probability of a new business succeeding.\n\nSports: The probability of a football team winning a match.\n\nInsurance: Companies calculate the probability of accidents to set prices.\n\nProbability helps us make better decisions when we face uncertainty.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: The weather forecast says 60% chance of rain. Should you carry an umbrella?\nSolution:\nYes! 60% is more than half, so rain is more likely than not. Carry an umbrella.\n\nExample 2: A farmer knows from 10 years of records that in 7 years, the rainy season started in March. What is the probability it will start in March this year?\nSolution:\nExperimental P(March) = 7/10 = 0.7 = 70%\nThis is useful for planning when to plant crops.\n\nExample 3: A multiple choice question has 4 options. You guess. What is the probability of getting it right?\nSolution:\nP(correct) = 1/4 = 0.25 = 25%. So guessing is not a good strategy!",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) The forecast says 20% chance of rain. Should you take an umbrella? Why?\n2. (Easy) A student studied 8 out of 10 topics. If one topic is chosen at random for the test, P(she studied it) = ?\n3. (Medium) A bus arrives on time 85 out of 100 days. What is the probability it arrives on time tomorrow?\n4. (Medium) A farmer plants 200 seeds. 180 germinate. What is the probability a seed germinates?\n5. (Hard) A company makes phones. 5 out of every 500 phones are faulty. What is the probability a phone is faulty? If you buy 3 phones, how many would you expect to be faulty?",
                  "summary": "* Probability helps in weather, farming, health, business and daily decisions\n* Use past data to predict future events\n* Probability guides decision-making under uncertainty\n* The higher the probability, the more likely the event",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "A bus arrives on time 72 out of 90 days. Experimental P(on time) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.7",
                      "B": "0.8",
                      "C": "0.85",
                      "D": "0.9"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  },
                  "practice_answers": "1. No, only 20%\n2. 8/10 = 0.8\n3. 0.85\n4. 0.9\n5. 0.01, expect 0 faulty in 3"
                }
              ],
