            {
              "name": "Unit 2: Functions",
              "lessons": [
                "Relations and functions",
                "Domain and range",
                "Linear functions and graphs",
                "Quadratic functions",
                "Applications of functions"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand what a relation is\n- Understand what a function is\n- Distinguish relations from functions",
                  "reading": "A relation connects inputs to outputs. A function is a special relation where each input has exactly ONE output.\n\nThink of a vending machine: one button gives one drink. That is a function!\n\nIn Rwanda, minutes talked (input) and cost (output) is a function: each time gives one cost.\n\nVertical line test: if a vertical line touches a graph more than once, it is NOT a function.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Is {(1,2), (3,4), (5,6)} a function?\nYes, each input has exactly one output.\n\nExample 2: Is {(1,2), (1,3), (2,4)} a function?\nNo, input 1 has two outputs: 2 and 3.\n\nExample 3: Is y = 2x + 1 a function?\nYes, each x gives exactly one y.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Is {(2,5), (3,7), (4,9)} a function? Why?\n2. (Easy) Is {(1,1), (1,2), (2,3)} a function? Why?\n3. (Medium) Does y = x² represent a function? Explain.\n4. (Medium) Does x = y² represent a function? (Hint: try x = 4)\n5. (Hard) A school assigns each student a unique ID. Is this a function? Explain.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Yes, each input has one output\n2. No, input 1 has two outputs\n3. Yes, each x gives one y\n4. No, x=4 gives y=2 or -2\n5. Yes, each student has one ID",
                  "summary": "* A relation connects inputs to outputs\n* A function: each input has exactly ONE output\n* Use the vertical line test on graphs\n* Many real-world relationships are functions",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Which of these is a function?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "{(1,2), (1,3)}",
                      "B": "{(2,4), (3,5), (2,6)}",
                      "C": "{(1,1), (2,1), (3,1)}",
                      "D": "{(1,2), (1,2)}"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find the domain of a function\n- Find the range of a function\n- Express domain and range using sets",
                  "reading": "Domain = all possible inputs (x-values)\nRange = all possible outputs (y-values)\n\nThink of a cow: what it eats (grass, hay) is the domain. What it produces (milk, meat) is the range.\n\nIn Rwanda, if a boda-boda charges 200 RWF/km up to 10 km:\nDomain: {0, 1, ..., 10}, Range: {0, 200, ..., 2,000}\n\nRestrictions: f(x) = 1/x has x ≠ 0. f(x) = √x has x ≥ 0.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Domain and range of {(1,5), (2,10), (3,15)}\nDomain: {1, 2, 3}, Range: {5, 10, 15}\n\nExample 2: f(x) = 2x + 1, domain {0, 1, 2, 3}. Range?\nf(0)=1, f(1)=3, f(2)=5, f(3)=7. Range: {1, 3, 5, 7}\n\nExample 3: What x cannot be for f(x) = 3/(x - 2)?\nx - 2 ≠ 0, so x ≠ 2",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find domain and range of {(2,4), (3,6), (5,10)}\n2. (Easy) f(x) = x + 3, domain {1, 2, 3}. Find range.\n3. (Medium) Find domain of f(x) = 5/(x - 1)\n4. (Medium) f(x) = 3x, domain {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}. Find range.\n5. (Hard) A taxi charges 1,000 RWF flagfall + 500 RWF/km up to 20 km. Domain and range as sets.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Domain: {2,3,5}, Range: {4,6,10}\n2. {4,5,6}\n3. x ≠ 1\n4. {0,3,6,9,12}\n5. Domain: {0,...,20}, Range: {1,000, 1,500, ..., 11,000}",
                  "summary": "* Domain = set of all inputs (x-values)\n* Range = set of all outputs (y-values)\n* Watch for: no division by zero, no negative square roots\n* Real-world functions have limited domains/ranges",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "f(x) = x + 2, domain {1, 2, 3}. Range is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "{1,2,3}",
                      "B": "{2,3,4}",
                      "C": "{3,4,5}",
                      "D": "{1,4,9}"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Identify linear functions\n- Graph linear functions\n- Find gradient and y-intercept",
                  "reading": "Linear functions: f(x) = mx + c (or y = mx + c)\nm = gradient (steepness), c = y-intercept (crosses y-axis)\n\nGraph is a straight line.\n\nIn Rwanda, a farmer plants 100 trees + 50 trees/day:\nf(d) = 50d + 100. m = 50, c = 100.\n\nPositive m: line goes up. Negative m: line goes down.\n\nTo graph: make a table, plot points, draw line.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: y = 2x + 1. Gradient m = 2, intercept c = 1\n\nExample 2: Table for y = 2x + 1, x = 0 to 3\nx: 0,1,2,3\ny: 1,3,5,7\n\nExample 3: Taxi charges 1,000 RWF + 500/km. Function?\nf(d) = 500d + 1,000",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Find m and c of y = 3x + 4\n2. (Easy) Find m and c of y = -2x + 7\n3. (Medium) Table for y = 3x - 1, x = 0 to 3\n4. (Medium) Bucket starts with 5 L, fills at 2 L/min. Write the function.\n5. (Hard) f(x) = -x + 3. Table for x = -2 to 2. Describe gradient.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. m = 3, c = 4\n2. m = -2, c = 7\n3. x:0,1,2,3 | y:-1,2,5,8\n4. f(t) = 2t + 5\n5. x:-2,-1,0,1,2 | y:5,4,3,2,1; negative gradient",
                  "summary": "* Linear functions: y = mx + c\n* m = gradient (steepness)\n* c = y-intercept\n* Positive m: line goes up, Negative m: goes down",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "The gradient of y = 4x - 3 is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "4",
                      "B": "-3",
                      "C": "3",
                      "D": "-4"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Identify quadratic functions\n- Graph simple quadratics\n- Understand vertex and parabola shape",
                  "reading": "Quadratic: f(x) = ax² + bx + c, a ≠ 0\nGraph is a U-shaped parabola.\na > 0: opens upward (smile). a < 0: opens downward (frown).\n\nIn Rwanda: path of a thrown ball, area of a field with fixed perimeter.\n\nThe turning point is the vertex. The vertical line through it is the axis of symmetry.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: f(x) = x², x = -3 to 3\nx: -3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\ny: 9,4,1,0,1,4,9\nOpens upward (a = 1 > 0)\n\nExample 2: f(x) = -2x² + 3x. Opens downward (a = -2 < 0)\n\nExample 3: Rectangle area A(x) = x(10 - x) = -x² + 10x\nOpens downward.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Is f(x) = 5x² + 2x - 1 a quadratic?\n2. (Easy) Does f(x) = x² + 3 open up or down?\n3. (Medium) Table for f(x) = x² - 2, x = -2 to 2\n4. (Medium) Does f(x) = -3x² + x open up or down?\n5. (Hard) Ball path: h(t) = -5t² + 20t + 1. Find h(0), h(1), h(2), h(3). When is it highest?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Yes\n2. Up (a = 1 > 0)\n3. x:-2,-1,0,1,2 | y:2,-1,-2,-1,2\n4. Down (a = -3 < 0)\n5. h(0)=1, h(1)=16, h(2)=21, h(3)=16; highest at t=2",
                  "summary": "* Quadratic: f(x) = ax² + bx + c (a ≠ 0)\n* Graph is a parabola (U-shape)\n* a > 0: opens up, a < 0: opens down\n* Vertex is the turning point",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "The graph of f(x) = x² - 4 opens:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Upward",
                      "B": "Downward",
                      "C": "Sideways",
                      "D": "Not a parabola"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply functions to real life\n- Interpret function graphs\n- Use functions for predictions",
                  "reading": "Functions model real-world situations!\n\nIn Rwanda: harvest depends on fertilizer, mobile money balance depends on deposits, temperature depends on time.\n\nFrom a graph we can see trends, make predictions, and find values.\n\nUnderstanding functions helps us make better decisions!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: d(t) = 15t (distance at 15 km/h). d(3) = 45 km. Time for 60 km? 15t = 60, t = 4 hours.\n\nExample 2: Factory: y = 200 + 50x phones/day. For 10 workers: 700 phones. Workers for 400 phones? 200 + 50x = 400, x = 4.\n\nExample 3: C(u) = 200u + 500 (water cost). C(8) = 2,100 RWF.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Farmer: y = 40x eggs/day from x chickens. Eggs from 25 chickens?\n2. (Easy) Using y = 40x, chickens for 200 eggs?\n3. (Medium) Car: f(d) = d/12 litres for d km. Fuel for 180 km?\n4. (Medium) Phone: C(m) = 300 + 100m for m minutes. Cost for 15 min?\n5. (Hard) Shop profit P(x) = 500x - 2,000. Find break-even (profit = 0).",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 1,000 eggs\n2. 5 chickens\n3. 15 litres\n4. 1,800 RWF\n5. x = 4 items",
                  "summary": "* Functions model real-world relationships\n* Input = domain, output = range in context\n* Evaluate functions to solve problems\n* Graphs help visualize trends",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Profit P(x) = 2,000x - 10,000. Profit for 10 bags:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "5,000 RWF",
                      "B": "10,000 RWF",
                      "C": "20,000 RWF",
                      "D": "30,000 RWF"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                }
              ],
