            {
              "name": "Unit 4: Coordinate Geometry",
              "lessons": [
                "Cartesian plane",
                "Distance between two points",
                "Midpoint formula",
                "Gradient of a line",
                "Equation of a straight line"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Locate points on the Cartesian plane\n- Identify x and y coordinates\n- Plot points from ordered pairs",
                  "reading": "Cartesian plane: x-axis (horizontal), y-axis (vertical). Meet at origin (0,0).\n\nEach point is (x, y): x = horizontal, y = vertical.\n\nIn Rwanda, like a map of Kigali with grid references.\n\n4 quadrants:\nI: (+,+), II: (-,+), III: (-,-), IV: (+,-)",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Plot A(2,3): 2 right, 3 up. B(-1,4): 1 left, 4 up.\n\nExample 2: Origin = (0,0)\n\nExample 3: (-3,-5) is Quadrant III (both negative)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Coordinates of point 3 right, 2 up?\n2. (Easy) Quadrant of (4, -2)?\n3. (Medium) Plot A(1,2), B(-2,3), C(-1,-1), D(2,-3)\n4. (Medium) Coordinates of point 5 left, 0 up/down?\n5. (Hard) Square at (1,1), (4,1), (1,4), (4,4). Side length?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. (3, 2)\n2. Quadrant IV\n3. (Plot)\n4. (-5, 0)\n5. Side = 3 units",
                  "summary": "* Cartesian plane: x-axis (horizontal), y-axis (vertical)\n* Ordered pair (x, y): x first, then y\n* Origin = (0, 0)\n* 4 quadrants by signs of x and y",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Point (-2, 5) is in quadrant:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "I",
                      "B": "II",
                      "C": "III",
                      "D": "IV"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate distance between two points\n- Use the distance formula\n- Apply distance to real problems",
                  "reading": "Distance = √[(x₂ - x₁)² + (y₂ - y₁)²]\n\nBased on Pythagoras' theorem.\n\nFrom (1,2) to (4,6):\nHorizontal: 3, Vertical: 4\nDistance: √(9+16) = √25 = 5\n\nIf same x: just y difference. If same y: just x difference.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: (1,2) to (4,6): d = √[(4-1)²+(6-2)²] = 5\n\nExample 2: (-2,3) to (3,-1): d = √[25+16] = √41 ≈ 6.4\n\nExample 3: Schools at (0,0) and (3,4): d = √(9+16) = 5 km",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Distance between (0,0) and (3,4)\n2. (Easy) Distance between (1,2) and (1,7)\n3. (Medium) Distance between (-3,2) and (2,-2)\n4. (Medium) Market at (2,1), school at (5,5). Distance?\n5. (Hard) Show A(0,0), B(3,0), C(0,4) form a right triangle using distance formula.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 5\n2. 5\n3. √41 ≈ 6.4\n4. 5 units\n5. AB=3, AC=4, BC=5; 3²+4²=5²",
                  "summary": "* Distance: d = √[(x₂-x₁)²+(y₂-y₁)²]\n* Based on Pythagoras\n* Same x: just y diff. Same y: just x diff.",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Distance between (0,0) and (6,8) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "7",
                      "B": "9",
                      "C": "10",
                      "D": "12"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find midpoint of a line segment\n- Use the midpoint formula\n- Apply midpoint to real problems",
                  "reading": "Midpoint = ((x₁ + x₂)/2, (y₁ + y₂)/2)\n\nThe point exactly halfway between two points.\n\nIn Rwanda: field corner (0,0) to corner (100,60), center = (50,30).\n\nWe can also find one endpoint given midpoint and the other endpoint.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Midpoint of (2,4) and (6,8)\nx: (2+6)/2 = 4, y: (4+8)/2 = 6\nMidpoint = (4,6)\n\nExample 2: (-3,5) and (7,-1)\nx: (-3+7)/2 = 2, y: (5-1)/2 = 2\nMidpoint = (2,2)\n\nExample 3: Road (10,20) to (50,40). Halfway?\n((10+50)/2, (20+40)/2) = (30,30)",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Midpoint of (0,0) and (4,6)\n2. (Easy) Midpoint of (3,7) and (9,1)\n3. (Medium) Midpoint of (-4,2) and (6,-8)\n4. (Medium) Tap at (5,2), field at (13,10). New tap halfway?\n5. (Hard) Endpoint (2,3), midpoint (5,8). Other endpoint?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. (2, 3)\n2. (6, 4)\n3. (1, -3)\n4. (9, 6)\n5. (8, 13)",
                  "summary": "* Midpoint = average of coordinates\n* ((x₁+x₂)/2, (y₁+y₂)/2)\n* Useful for centers and halfway points",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Midpoint of (1,3) and (7,5) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "(3,4)",
                      "B": "(4,4)",
                      "C": "(4,5)",
                      "D": "(5,4)"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find gradient of a line\n- Understand positive, negative, zero gradients\n- Calculate gradient from two points",
                  "reading": "Gradient (slope) = rise/run = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁)\n\nClimbing a hill in Rwanda:\n- Steep hill = large gradient\n- Flat ground = zero gradient\n- Downhill = negative gradient\n\nPositive: line goes up. Negative: line goes down.\nZero: horizontal. Undefined: vertical (divide by zero).",
                  "examples": "Example 1: (1,2) to (4,8): rise=6, run=3, m=2\n\nExample 2: (3,7) to (5,3): rise=-4, run=2, m=-2\n\nExample 3: Road rises 20 m over 100 m: m=20/100=0.2",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Gradient between (0,0) and (2,6)\n2. (Easy) Gradient between (1,5) and (4,5)\n3. (Medium) Gradient between (-2,4) and (3,-1)\n4. (Medium) Hill rises 50 m over 200 m. Gradient?\n5. (Hard) Gradient between (2,3) and (5,y) is 4. Find y.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 3\n2. 0 (horizontal)\n3. -1\n4. 0.25 or 1/4\n5. y = 15",
                  "summary": "* Gradient = rise/run = change y / change x\n* m = (y₂ - y₁)/(x₂ - x₁)\n* Positive: up, Negative: down, Zero: horizontal",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Gradient between (0,0) and (4,12) is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "2",
                      "B": "3",
                      "C": "4",
                      "D": "6"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find equation of a straight line\n- Use y = mx + c\n- Find equation from two points or gradient + point",
                  "reading": "Equation: y = mx + c\nm = gradient, c = y-intercept\n\nIn Rwanda: taxi charges 1,000 RWF + 500/km: y = 500x + 1,000\n\nGiven gradient and point: substitute to find c.\nGiven two points: find m first, then find c.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: m = 2, c = 3. Equation: y = 2x + 3\n\nExample 2: Through (1,5), m = 3\ny = 3x + c. 5 = 3(1) + c, c = 2. y = 3x + 2\n\nExample 3: Through (2,7) and (4,13)\nm = (13-7)/(4-2) = 3\ny = 3x + c. 7 = 3(2) + c, c = 1. y = 3x + 1",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Equation with m = 4, c = 1\n2. (Easy) Equation with m = -2, c = 5\n3. (Medium) Through (0,2), m = 3\n4. (Medium) Through (1,6) and (3,10)\n5. (Hard) Through (-2,1) and (3,-4). Find m and equation.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. y = 4x + 1\n2. y = -2x + 5\n3. y = 3x + 2\n4. y = 2x + 4\n5. m = -1, y = -x - 1",
                  "summary": "* Equation: y = mx + c\n* m = gradient, c = y-intercept\n* Given gradient + point: find c\n* Given two points: find m first",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Line through (0,3) with gradient 2:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "y = 2x + 3",
                      "B": "y = 3x + 2",
                      "C": "y = 2x - 3",
                      "D": "y = -2x + 3"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "previous_answers": "Unit 3 Quiz Answers: 1-B, 2-B, 3-B, 4-A, 5-B, 6-B, 7-B, 8-C, 9-C, 10-C",
