            {
              "name": "Unit 5: Similarity and Congruence",
              "lessons": [
                "Congruent triangles",
                "Similar triangles",
                "Scale factors",
                "Applications of similarity",
                "Geometric proofs"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand congruent triangles\n- Identify congruence using SSS, SAS, ASA, RHS\n- Write congruence statements",
                  "reading": "Congruent triangles: exactly same size and shape.\nTests:\n- SSS: all 3 sides equal\n- SAS: 2 sides + included angle equal\n- ASA: 2 angles + included side equal\n- RHS: right angle + hypotenuse + one side\n\n△ABC ≡ △DEF means they are congruent.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Both triangles have sides 5, 6, 7. Congruent by SSS.\n\nExample 2: PQ=8, PR=6, ∠P=60° and ST=8, SU=6, ∠S=60°. Congruent by SAS.\n\nExample 3: Right triangles with sides 3, 4, 5. Congruent by RHS.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Triangle A: 4,5,6. Triangle B: 6,4,5. Congruent? Why?\n2. (Easy) Which test checks all three sides?\n3. (Medium) Same three angles but different sides. Congruent?\n4. (Medium) Two right triangles: both hyp=10, one side=6. Congruent? Why?\n5. (Hard) AB=DE, BC=EF, ∠B=∠E. Prove congruent. Which test?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Yes, SSS\n2. SSS\n3. No, similar but not congruent\n4. Yes, RHS\n5. Yes, SAS",
                  "summary": "* Congruent: same size and shape\n* Tests: SSS, SAS, ASA, RHS\n* ≡ symbol for congruence",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Test that proves all three sides equal:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "SAS",
                      "B": "ASA",
                      "C": "SSS",
                      "D": "RHS"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand similar triangles\n- Use similarity to find unknown lengths\n- Understand scale factors",
                  "reading": "Similar triangles: same shape, different size.\n- Corresponding angles equal\n- Sides proportional (same scale factor)\n\n△ABC ~ △DEF means similar.\n\nPhoto vs life-size: same shape, different size!\n\nScale factor = new/original.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Sides 4,6,8 and 6,9,12. Scale factor = 6/4 = 1.5\n\nExample 2: △PQR ~ △STU, PQ=5, ST=15. Scale = 3.\n\nExample 3: 2 m stick, 0.5 m shadow. Tree shadow 10 m. Height?\nflagpole/2 = 10/0.5 = 20. Height = 40 m",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Sides 3,4,5 and 6,8,10. Similar? Scale?\n2. (Easy) In similar triangles, what is ALWAYS equal?\n3. (Medium) △ABC ~ △DEF. AB=4, DE=10. BC=6. Find EF.\n4. (Medium) 1.5 m person, 2 m shadow. Tree shadow 12 m. Tree height?\n5. (Hard) Areas 25 cm² and 100 cm². Scale factor? (Area scale = length²)",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Yes, scale = 2\n2. Corresponding angles\n3. EF = 15\n4. 9 m\n5. Scale factor = 2",
                  "summary": "* Similar: same shape, proportional sides\n* Scale factor = new/original\n* Corresponding angles equal\n* Use ratios to find unknowns",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Scale factor from 3 to 9 is:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "1/3",
                      "B": "3",
                      "C": "6",
                      "D": "9"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Use scale factors to find unknown measurements\n- Apply similarity to maps and models\n- Calculate lengths using proportion",
                  "reading": "Scale factor k:\n- Lengths: multiply by k\n- Areas: multiply by k²\n- Volumes: multiply by k³\n\nModel at 1:50. 20 cm model = 20×50 = 1,000 cm = 10 m real.\nFloor area 0.5 m² model = 0.5×2,500 = 1,250 m² real.\n\nUsed in construction, maps, and design!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Map 1:10,000. 8 cm apart. Real = 8×10,000 = 80,000 cm = 0.8 km\n\nExample 2: Photo 15 cm, enlarged 3x. New = 45 cm. Area = 9x original.\n\nExample 3: Model car 30 cm at 1:20. Real = 600 cm = 6 m.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Plan 1:100, room 5 cm. Real length?\n2. (Easy) Scale 4, original 7 cm. New?\n3. (Medium) Map 1:50,000, 15 cm. Real distance in km?\n4. (Medium) Triangle 3,4,5 scaled by 3. New sides?\n5. (Hard) Square side 4 cm, scale 2.5. New side, new area, ratio?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 500 cm = 5 m\n2. 28 cm\n3. 7.5 km\n4. 9, 12, 15\n5. Side=10 cm, Area=100 cm², ratio=6.25:1",
                  "summary": "* Length: ×k, Area: ×k², Volume: ×k³\n* Maps and models use scale factors\n* Useful in construction and design",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Scale 5, original 8 cm. New length:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "13 cm",
                      "B": "25 cm",
                      "C": "40 cm",
                      "D": "64 cm"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply similarity to real situations\n- Solve indirect measurement problems\n- Use similar triangles for heights and distances",
                  "reading": "Practical uses in Rwanda:\n1. Shadow method: similar triangles find heights\n2. Maps: scale gives real distances\n3. Construction: scale plans become real buildings\n4. Quality control: identical = congruent products",
                  "examples": "Example 1: 2 m pole, 3 m shadow. Building 15 m shadow. Height? 15×2/3 = 10 m\n\nExample 2: Map 1 cm = 5 km. Kigali-Butare 16 cm. Real = 80 km\n\nExample 3: Frame 20×30 cm, similar frame 50 cm wide. Height? 30×2.5 = 75 cm",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) 1 m stick, 1.5 m shadow. Tree 6 m shadow. Height?\n2. (Easy) Map 1 cm = 2 km. 9 cm apart. Real distance?\n3. (Medium) Photo 10×15 cm, enlarged to 25 cm wide. New height?\n4. (Medium) 1.8 m person, 2.4 m shadow. Flagpole 8 m shadow. Height?\n5. (Hard) Map 1:100,000, lake 4 cm². Real area in km²?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 4 m\n2. 18 km\n3. 37.5 cm\n4. 6 m\n5. 4 km²",
                  "summary": "* Use similar triangles for indirect measurement\n* Map scales give real distances\n* Scales apply to lengths, areas, volumes",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "1.5 m stick, 2 m shadow. Tree 10 m shadow. Height:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "5 m",
                      "B": "6.5 m",
                      "C": "7.5 m",
                      "D": "8 m"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Write simple geometric proofs\n- Use congruence and similarity in reasoning\n- Justify geometric statements logically",
                  "reading": "Geometric proofs: step-by-step logical arguments with reasons.\n\nLike explaining why two triangles are identical:\n1. AB = DE (given)\n2. ∠A = ∠D (alternate angles)\n3. △ABC ≡ △DEF (ASA)\n\nAlways state: what you know AND why you know it!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: AB=DE, BC=EF, ∠B=∠E. Prove congruence.\n1. AB=DE (given)\n2. BC=EF (given)\n3. ∠B=∠E (given)\n4. △ABC≡△DEF (SAS)\n\nExample 2: Angles 60° and 80°. Prove third = 40°.\nTriangle sum = 180°, so 60+80+x=180, x=40°\n\nExample 3: AB=AC in triangle. Prove ∠B=∠C.\nIsosceles triangle theorem: base angles equal.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) PQ=RS, QR=SP. Prove △PQR≡△RSP. Which test?\n2. (Easy) Sum of angles in a triangle?\n3. (Medium) Angles 45° and 90°. Prove third = 45°.\n4. (Medium) AB=5, BC=7, ∠B=50°. DE=5, EF=7, ∠E=50°. Prove congruent.\n5. (Hard) AD⊥BC, BD=DC. Prove AB=AC.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. SSS\n2. 180°\n3. 180-(45+90) = 45°\n4. SAS\n5. △ABD≡△ACD (SAS), so AB=AC",
                  "summary": "* Proofs use logical steps with reasons\n* Start with given information\n* Use known theorems and congruence tests\n* Each step must be justified",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Two sides and included angle equal: use test:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "SSS",
                      "B": "SAS",
                      "C": "ASA",
                      "D": "RHS"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                }
              ],
