            {
              "name": "Unit 6: Trigonometry",
              "lessons": [
                "Trigonometric ratios (sine, cosine, tangent)",
                "Solving right triangles",
                "Angle of elevation and depression",
                "Applications of trigonometry",
                "Trigonometric tables"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand the three trig ratios\n- Identify opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse\n- Write sin, cos, tan ratios",
                  "reading": "Trigonometry relates angles to sides in right triangles.\n\nSOH CAH TOA:\nsin(θ) = Opposite/Hypotenuse\ncos(θ) = Adjacent/Hypotenuse\ntan(θ) = Opposite/Adjacent\n\nHypotenuse = longest side, opposite the right angle.\nOpposite = opposite the angle. Adjacent = next to the angle.\n\nUsed in Rwanda for building, surveying, navigation.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Angle 30°, opposite=3, hyp=6. sin(30°)=3/6=0.5\n\nExample 2: Angle 45°, opp=5, adj=5. tan(45°)=5/5=1\n\nExample 3: For angle A: opp=4, adj=3, hyp=5\nsin(A)=4/5=0.8, cos(A)=3/5=0.6, tan(A)=4/3≈1.33",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Angle 60°, opp=8, hyp=16. sin(60°)?\n2. (Easy) For angle B, adj=6, hyp=10. cos(B)?\n3. (Medium) Triangle 5,12,13. For angle opposite side 5, find sin, cos, tan.\n4. (Medium) sin(θ)=3/5, hyp=10. Find opposite.\n5. (Hard) tan(θ)=2, adj=4. Find opposite and hyp.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 0.5\n2. 0.6\n3. sin=5/13, cos=12/13, tan=5/12\n4. Opposite = 6\n5. Opp=8, hyp=√80≈8.94",
                  "summary": "* sin(θ) = Opp/Hyp, cos(θ) = Adj/Hyp, tan(θ) = Opp/Adj\n* SOH CAH TOA memory trick\n* Hypotenuse is longest side",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "tan(θ) = opposite / ?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "hypotenuse",
                      "B": "adjacent",
                      "C": "opposite",
                      "D": "sum of sides"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Use trig ratios to find unknown sides\n- Choose the correct ratio\n- Solve right triangles for missing sides",
                  "reading": "Steps to find missing side:\n1. Label opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse\n2. Choose correct ratio (SOH CAH TOA)\n3. Write equation\n4. Solve\n\nExample: ladder at 60°, reaches 5 m. Length?\nsin(60°) = 5/h, h = 5/0.866 ≈ 5.77 m\n\nAlways check: answer should make sense!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Angle 30°, adj=10. Find opp.\ntan(30°)=opp/10, 0.577=opp/10, opp≈5.77\n\nExample 2: Ladder 70°, base 2 m from wall. Length?\ncos(70°)=2/h, 0.342=2/h, h≈5.85 m\n\nExample 3: Kite string 50 m at 40°. Height?\nsin(40°)=opp/50, 0.643=opp/50, opp≈32.15 m",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) sin(30°)=x/10. Find x.\n2. (Easy) cos(60°)=5/x. Find x.\n3. (Medium) Angle 35°, adj=8. Find opp. (tan35°≈0.700)\n4. (Medium) 10 m ladder at 65°. Height up wall? (sin65°≈0.906)\n5. (Hard) 30 m from tree, angle 55°. Height? (tan55°≈1.428)",
                  "practice_answers": "1. x = 5\n2. x = 10\n3. Opp ≈ 5.6\n4. Height ≈ 9.06 m\n5. Height ≈ 42.8 m",
                  "summary": "* Label sides from the given angle\n* Choose ratio linking known and unknown\n* Solve and check answer is reasonable",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "To find opposite from adjacent and angle, use:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "sin",
                      "B": "cos",
                      "C": "tan",
                      "D": "Pythagoras"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand angle of elevation\n- Understand angle of depression\n- Solve real problems with these angles",
                  "reading": "Angle of elevation: looking UP from horizontal.\nAngle of depression: looking DOWN from horizontal.\n\nIn Rwanda: looking up at a hilltop, or down from a hill.\n\nThese angles are EQUAL when looking between two points (alternate angles).\n\nMeasured from horizontal, never vertical!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: 20 m from tree, angle 40°. Height?\ntan(40°)=h/20, 0.839=h/20, h=16.8 m\n\nExample 2: 30 m building, depression 25° to car. Distance?\ntan(25°)=30/d, 0.466=30/d, d≈64.4 m\n\nExample 3: Hill 50 m, depression 30° to lake. Distance?\ntan(30°)=50/d, 0.577=50/d, d≈86.6 m",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) 10 m away, elevation 30°. Height? (tan30°≈0.577)\n2. (Easy) Depression 45° from 20 m tower. Distance? (tan45°=1)\n3. (Medium) 15 m tree, 25 m away. Angle of elevation?\n4. (Medium) 100 m cliff, depression 20° to boat. Distance? (tan20°≈0.364)\n5. (Hard) 40 m hill: car at 35° depression, moves closer to 50°. Distance moved?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 5.77 m\n2. 20 m\n3. ≈ 31°\n4. ≈ 274.7 m\n5. Initial 57.1 m, final 33.6 m, moved 23.5 m",
                  "summary": "* Elevation: looking UP. Depression: looking DOWN.\n* These angles are equal between two points\n* Use tan = opposite/adjacent",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Angle measured UP from horizontal:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Depression",
                      "B": "Elevation",
                      "C": "Reflex",
                      "D": "Obtuse"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply trigonometry to real problems\n- Solve height and distance problems\n- Use trig in construction and navigation",
                  "reading": "Trig is everywhere!\n\nIn Rwanda:\n- Building roof slopes\n- Land surveying (measure without crossing rivers!)\n- Road gradients through hills\n- Sports: kicking angles\n\nTrig turns hard-to-measure distances into simple calculations!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Hill top at 25°, 100 m away. Height = 100×tan25° = 46.6 m\n\nExample 2: Roof rise 3 m, run 4 m. Angle? tan(θ)=3/4=0.75, θ≈36.9°\n\nExample 3: 5 m ramp rises 1.5 m. Angle? sin(θ)=1.5/5=0.3, θ≈17.5°",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Ramp rises 1 m over 5 m. Angle? (tan⁻¹)\n2. (Easy) 6 m ladder reaches 4 m up. Angle with wall?\n3. (Medium) 200 m hill, depression 15°. Horizontal distance? (tan15°≈0.268)\n4. (Medium) Road rises 50 m over 400 m. Gradient and angle.\n5. (Hard) Tree: angle 32° from A, 20 m closer angle 45°. Height?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. ≈ 11.3°\n2. ≈ 48.2°\n3. ≈ 746.3 m\n4. Gradient=0.125, angle≈7.1°\n5. h/d=tan32°, h/(d-20)=tan45°; h≈32.3 m",
                  "summary": "* Trig solves height and distance problems\n* Angle of elevation/depression are practical tools\n* Draw a diagram first!",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Ramp rises 2 m over 10 m. Gradient:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.1",
                      "B": "0.2",
                      "C": "0.5",
                      "D": "5"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Read trigonometric tables\n- Use a calculator for trig values\n- Find angles from known ratios",
                  "reading": "Trig tables: sin, cos, tan for 0° to 90°.\n\nTo find sin(30°): look at sin column at 30° = 0.5000\nTo find angle when sin(θ)=0.5: find 0.5 in sin column, read 30°\n\nInverse functions (sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹, tan⁻¹) find angle from ratio.\n\nKey values:\nsin(0°)=0, sin(30°)=0.5, sin(90°)=1\ncos(0°)=1, cos(60°)=0.5, cos(90°)=0\ntan(0°)=0, tan(45°)=1",
                  "examples": "Example 1: From table, tan(35°) ≈ 0.700\n\nExample 2: sin(θ)=0.866. Table shows 60°. θ = 60°\n\nExample 3: tan(θ)=1.732. Table shows 60°. θ = 60°",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) From memory: sin(30°)?\n2. (Easy) cos(60°)?\n3. (Medium) Find θ if tan(θ)=1\n4. (Medium) sin(θ)=0.342. θ? (sin20°≈0.342)\n5. (Hard) Triangle: opp=5, adj=12. Find angle using tan⁻¹.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 0.5\n2. 0.5\n3. 45°\n4. 20°\n5. tan(θ)=5/12≈0.417, θ≈22.6°",
                  "summary": "* Trig tables give sin, cos, tan for 0-90°\n* Use inverse to find angles\n* Key: sin30=0.5, tan45=1",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "sin(30°) equals:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0",
                      "B": "0.5",
                      "C": "1",
                      "D": "0.866"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                }
              ],
