            {
              "name": "Unit 8: Statistics",
              "lessons": [
                "Frequency distribution tables",
                "Histograms and frequency polygons",
                "Measures of central tendency",
                "Measures of dispersion",
                "Cumulative frequency curves"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Create frequency distribution tables\n- Group data into class intervals\n- Understand frequency",
                  "reading": "Frequency = how many times a value occurs.\n\nShoe sizes: 6,7,7,8,7,6,8,9,7,8\nTable:\nSize | Freq\n6 | 2\n7 | 4\n8 | 3\n9 | 1\n\nGroup data into equal-width intervals when many different values exist.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Frequency table for 2,3,2,4,3,2,5,4,3,2\n2:4, 3:3, 4:2, 5:1, Total:10\n\nExample 2: Group marks into 0-29, 30-59, 60-100\n45,78,23,90,56,34,67,88,12,45\n0-29:2, 30-59:4, 60-100:4\n\nExample 3: Grouping makes large data easier to understand.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Frequency table: A,B,A,C,B,A,C,C,B,A\n2. (Easy) Frequency of 'A' in Q1?\n3. (Medium) Ages: 5,7,12,15,8,10,25,30,18,22,6,14. Group 0-9,10-19,20-29,30-39.\n4. (Medium) Heights: 150,155,160,150,165,155,160,150,170,155. Frequency table.\n5. (Hard) Mangoes: 12,15,10,8,20,15,12,10,25,18,15,12. Group into 8-11,12-15,16-19,20-25.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. A:4, B:3, C:3\n2. 4\n3. 0-9:4, 10-19:4, 20-29:2, 30-39:2\n4. 150:3, 155:3, 160:2, 165:1, 170:1\n5. 8-11:2, 12-15:5, 16-19:2, 20-25:3",
                  "summary": "* Frequency = count of occurrences\n* Tables organize raw data\n* Group equal-width intervals for large data\n* Reveals patterns",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "In a frequency table, 'frequency' means:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "The values",
                      "B": "How many times a value appears",
                      "C": "The average",
                      "D": "The largest value"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Draw histograms\n- Draw frequency polygons\n- Interpret graphs",
                  "reading": "Histogram: bars touching, for grouped numerical data.\nHeight = frequency, width = class interval.\n\nFrequency polygon: line connecting midpoints.\nMidpoint = (lower+upper)/2\n\nIn Rwanda: school test score distribution.\n\nBar chart has gaps (categorical). Histogram has no gaps (numerical).",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Histogram: Age 0-9:5, 10-19:8, 20-29:4, 30-39:3\nBars touching, heights 5,8,4,3\n\nExample 2: Midpoints for above:\n4.5, 14.5, 24.5, 34.5\nPlot and connect: (4.5,5), (14.5,8), (24.5,4), (34.5,3)\n\nExample 3: Bar chart = categorical data with gaps.\nHistogram = numerical data, bars touch.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) In a histogram, bars touch or have gaps?\n2. (Easy) Midpoint of interval 10-20?\n3. (Medium) Histogram: 0-10(4), 10-20(7), 20-30(9), 30-40(5)\n4. (Medium) Midpoints for: 5-9, 10-14, 15-19?\n5. (Hard) Frequency polygon: Heights 140-149(3), 150-159(8), 160-169(7), 170-179(2)",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Touch (no gaps)\n2. 15\n3. (Histogram heights: 4,7,9,5)\n4. 7, 12, 17\n5. Points at (144.5,3), (154.5,8), (164.5,7), (174.5,2)",
                  "summary": "* Histogram: bars touching, grouped numerical data\n* Frequency polygon: line through midpoints\n* Midpoint = (lower+upper)/2",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "In a histogram, bars:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Have gaps",
                      "B": "Touch each other",
                      "C": "Are circles",
                      "D": "Show percentages"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Calculate mean, median, mode\n- Choose best average\n- Interpret central tendency",
                  "reading": "Mean = sum ÷ count (affected by outliers).\nMedian = middle value when ordered (not affected by outliers).\nMode = most frequent value.\n\nIn Rwanda:\nMean: average daily income\nMedian: typical house price (outliers don't affect)\nMode: most common crop",
                  "examples": "Example 1: 4,7,3,7,9. Mean=6, Med=7, Mode=7\n\nExample 2: Even count: 2,3,5,8,9,10. Med=(5+8)/2=6.5\n\nExample 3: Cow weights 200,220,240,260,280. Mean=240 kg",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Mean of 5,10,15,20?\n2. (Easy) Mode of 2,3,2,4,5,2,6?\n3. (Medium) Mean, median, mode of 12,15,12,18,20,15,12\n4. (Medium) Salaries: 200,210,220,230,500 (1,000 RWF). Best average? Why?\n5. (Hard) Mean of 5 numbers = 12. Four: 10,14,8,16. Find fifth.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 12.5\n2. 2\n3. Mean=14.9, Med=15, Mode=12\n4. Median (220) better, mean pulled up by 500\n5. Fifth = 12",
                  "summary": "* Mean = sum/count\n* Median = middle value\n* Mode = most frequent\n* Choose based on data and purpose",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Median of 3,7,8,10,12:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "7",
                      "B": "8",
                      "C": "9",
                      "D": "10"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand range and IQR\n- Understand data spread\n- Interpret dispersion measures",
                  "reading": "Range = largest - smallest (simple but affected by outliers).\nIQR = Q3 - Q1 (middle 50% spread).\nQ1 = median of lower half.\nQ3 = median of upper half.\n\nLarge spread = scattered data. Small spread = clustered data.\n\nIn Rwanda: temp range in Kigali ≈ 10-30°C, range=20°C.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: 10,15,22,30,45. Range=45-10=35\n\nExample 2: 4,7,9,12,15,18,21\nMedian=12, Q1=7, Q3=18, IQR=11\n\nExample 3: Class A range 10-20, Class B range 5-20. A is more consistent.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Range of 3,8,12,20,25?\n2. (Easy) Q1=15, Q3=30. IQR?\n3. (Medium) Median, Q1, Q3, IQR: 5,8,10,12,15,18,20,22\n4. (Medium) Class A mean=60, range=30. B mean=60, range=70. More consistent?\n5. (Hard) 10,15,20,25,30,35. Range, Q1, Q3, IQR.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 22\n2. 15\n3. Med=13.5, Q1=9, Q3=19, IQR=10\n4. Class A\n5. Range=25, Q1=15, Q3=30, IQR=15",
                  "summary": "* Range = max - min\n* IQR = Q3 - Q1 (middle 50%)\n* Small dispersion = consistent data",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "IQR stands for:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Inner Quartile Range",
                      "B": "Inter Quartile Range",
                      "C": "Interquartile Range",
                      "D": "Interval Quartile Range"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Draw cumulative frequency tables\n- Draw ogives\n- Find median and quartiles from graphs",
                  "reading": "Cumulative frequency = running total.\n\nSteps:\n1. Add frequencies cumulatively\n2. Plot cumulative freq against upper bound\n3. Connect with smooth curve (ogive)\n\nFrom ogive: Median at 50%, Q1 at 25%, Q3 at 75%.\n\nUsed in Rwanda to see how many scored below a mark.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Marks 0-20:5, 20-40:8, 40-60:12, 60-80:7, 80-100:3\nCum Freq: 5,13,25,32,35\n\nExample 2: From ogive, total=35. Median at 17.5th value.\nOn graph, read across from 17.5 to the curve, then down.≈ 45 marks\n\nExample 3: Q1 at 8.75th, Q3 at 26.25th.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Cumulative frequency means?\n2. (Easy) What is an ogive?\n3. (Medium) Build cum freq: 0-10:4, 10-20:7, 20-30:9, 30-40:5\n4. (Medium) From Q3, total=40. Find Q3 position.\n5. (Hard) Draw data: 0-20:3, 20-40:7, 40-60:10, 60-80:6, 80-100:4. Total? Median position?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Running total of frequencies\n2. Cumulative frequency curve\n3. 4, 11, 20, 25\n4. 30th value (75% of 40)\n5. Total=30, Median at 15th value",
                  "summary": "* Cumulative freq = running total\n* Ogive = cumulative frequency curve\n* Read median, Q1, Q3 from ogive",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Cumulative frequency curve is called:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "Histogram",
                      "B": "Ogive",
                      "C": "Bar chart",
                      "D": "Pictograph"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                }
              ],
