            {
              "name": "Unit 9: Probability",
              "lessons": [
                "Probability of combined events",
                "Tree diagrams",
                "Sample space for multiple events",
                "Experimental vs theoretical probability",
                "Applications of probability"
              ],
              "lessons_content": [
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find probability of combined events\n- Use 'and' and 'or' rules\n- Solve multi-event probability problems",
                  "reading": "For combined events:\nP(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) (independent events)\nP(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) (mutually exclusive)\n\nIn Rwanda: probability of rain AND carrying an umbrella.\nOr probability of choosing a boy OR a girl from a class.\n\nIndependent: one event doesn't affect the other.\nMutually exclusive: can't happen together.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Coin flip AND dice roll. P(heads AND 6)?\nP(heads)=1/2, P(6)=1/6\nP(heads AND 6) = 1/2 × 1/6 = 1/12\n\nExample 2: Bag: 3 red, 5 blue. P(red OR blue)?\nP(red)=3/8, P(blue)=5/8, mutually exclusive\nP(red OR blue) = 3/8 + 5/8 = 1 (certain!)\n\nExample 3: Dice: P(2 OR 5)? P(2)=1/6, P(5)=1/6, P=2/6=1/3",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Coin P(heads) AND coin P(heads)?\n2. (Easy) Dice P(3) OR P(4)?\n3. (Medium) Bag 2 red, 3 blue, 5 green. P(red OR blue)?\n4. (Medium) Two coins. P(both heads)?\n5. (Hard) Dice and coin together. P(even number AND heads)?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4\n2. 1/6 + 1/6 = 1/3\n3. 2/10 + 3/10 = 1/2\n4. 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4\n5. 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/4",
                  "summary": "* P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) (independent)\n* P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) (mutually exclusive)\n* Identify if events are independent or mutually exclusive",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "P(heads AND heads) with two coins:",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "1/4",
                      "B": "1/2",
                      "C": "1",
                      "D": "0"
                    },
                    "answer": "A"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Draw tree diagrams\n- Use tree diagrams for probability\n- Find probabilities of sequences",
                  "reading": "Tree diagrams show all possible outcomes.\n\nEach branch shows a probability.\nMultiply along branches for combined probability.\n\nIn Rwanda: a farmer deciding whether to plant maize or beans in two fields - tree diagram shows all combinations!\n\nTree diagrams help organize thinking about multi-step events.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Two coin flips tree:\nH (1/2) → H (1/2) = P(HH)=1/4\n     → T (1/2) = P(HT)=1/4\nT (1/2) → H (1/2) = P(TH)=1/4\n     → T (1/2) = P(TT)=1/4\n\nExample 2: Bag: 3 red, 2 blue. Pick twice with replacement.\nP(R,R) = 3/5 × 3/5 = 9/25\nP(R,B) = 3/5 × 2/5 = 6/25\nP(B,R) = 2/5 × 3/5 = 6/25\nP(B,B) = 2/5 × 2/5 = 4/25\nCheck sum: 25/25 = 1",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) How many branches for tossing 2 coins?\n2. (Easy) P(HH) from tree?\n3. (Medium) Draw tree for picking 2 marbles from 4 red + 6 blue (with replacement).\n4. (Medium) From tree in Q3, P(R,R)?\n5. (Hard) Bag: 5 red, 3 blue. Pick twice without replacement. Draw tree.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 4 branches\n2. 1/4\n3. (Tree: R(0.4)/B(0.6) each branch)\n4. 0.4 × 0.4 = 0.16\n5. (Tree: first R(5/8)/B(3/8); second depends on first)",
                  "summary": "* Tree diagrams show all outcomes\n* Multiply along branches\n* Sum of all outcomes = 1\n* Without replacement: probabilities change",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "How many outcomes for tossing 2 coins?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "2",
                      "B": "3",
                      "C": "4",
                      "D": "6"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Find sample spaces for multiple events\n- List all possible outcomes systematically\n- Count outcomes using tables",
                  "reading": "Sample space S = set of all possible outcomes.\n\nFor coin + dice: S = {H1,H2,H3,H4,H5,H6,T1,T2,T3,T4,T5,T6} = 12 outcomes\n\nWe can use tables to organize outcomes:\n   | 1 2 3 4 5 6\nH | H1 H2 H3 H4 H5 H6\nT | T1 T2 T3 T4 T5 T6\n\nTables help count and visualize!",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Two dice. How many outcomes?\n6 × 6 = 36 outcomes. Can list in a table!\n\nExample 2: Coin + coin (2 coins). S = {HH, HT, TH, TT} = 4\n\nExample 3: Spinner {A,B,C} + coin.\nS = {AH,AT,BH,BT,CH,CT} = 6 outcomes",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Sample space for rolling a dice?\n2. (Easy) How many outcomes for coin + coin?\n3. (Medium) List sample space for spinner {1,2,3} + coin.\n4. (Medium) Two dice: how many outcomes where sum = 7?\n5. (Hard) Make a table for coin + coin. List outcomes where at least one is heads.",
                  "practice_answers": "1. {1,2,3,4,5,6}\n2. 4\n3. {1H,1T,2H,2T,3H,3T}\n4. 6 outcomes: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)\n5. HH,HT,TH (3 outcomes)",
                  "summary": "* Sample space = all possible outcomes\n* Use tables to organize\n* Multiply to count total outcomes\n* Systematic listing avoids errors",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "Coin + coin: how many outcomes?",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "2",
                      "B": "3",
                      "C": "4",
                      "D": "6"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Understand experimental vs theoretical probability\n- Compare experiment results with theory\n- Explain why they may differ",
                  "reading": "Theoretical probability = what SHOULD happen (calculated).\nExperimental probability = what DOES happen (from experiment).\n\nLaw of Large Numbers: as trials increase, experimental probability gets closer to theoretical!\n\nIn Rwanda: flip a coin 10 times (experimental = maybe 6 heads). Flip 1,000 times (experimental ≈ 500 heads = theoretical 0.5).",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Coin flipped 20 times, 12 heads. Experimental P(H)=12/20=0.6. Theoretical=0.5.\n\nExample 2: Dice rolled 60 times, 8 sixes. P(6)=8/60≈0.133. Theoretical=1/6≈0.167.\n\nExample 3: Spin spinner 100 times: Red=30, Blue=40, Green=30. Is it fair? If fair, each = 33.3. Blue appears too often!",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) Coin 50 flips, 28 heads. Experimental P(H)?\n2. (Easy) Theoretical P(H) for a fair coin?\n3. (Medium) Dice 30 rolls, 4 sixes. Experimental vs theoretical P(6)?\n4. (Medium) Why do more trials give more accurate results?\n5. (Hard) Class A spins 10 times: Blue=6. Class B spins 100 times: Blue=52. Which class gives better estimate of P(Blue)? Why?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. 28/50 = 0.56\n2. 0.5\n3. Exp=4/30=0.133, Theory=1/6≈0.167\n4. More trials reduce random variation\n5. Class B (Law of Large Numbers)",
                  "summary": "* Theoretical = expected (calculated)\n* Experimental = observed (from trials)\n* More trials = closer to theoretical\n* Law of Large Numbers",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "50 flips, 28 heads. Experimental P(H):",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.5",
                      "B": "0.56",
                      "C": "0.6",
                      "D": "0.28"
                    },
                    "answer": "B"
                  }
                },
                {
                  "objectives": "By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: \n- Apply probability to real situations\n- Make predictions using probability\n- Understand risk and likelihood",
                  "reading": "Probability helps us make decisions!\n\nIn Rwanda:\n- Weather forecast: 80% chance of rain = take umbrella!\n- Farming: 90% germination rate = plant enough seeds\n- Business: 5% chance of product fault = quality control\n- Insurance: calculate risk and premiums\n\nProbability helps us understand risk and make informed choices.",
                  "examples": "Example 1: Forecast 20% rain. Should you take umbrella?\n20% = low chance, but if getting wet is bad, take it!\n\nExample 2: 200 seeds planted, 180 germinate. P(germinate) = 180/200 = 0.9. Plant 500 seeds? Expect 450 to germinate.\n\nExample 3: Factory: 5/500 phones faulty. P(faulty) = 0.01. Buy 3 phones? Expect 0.03 faulty, so likely 0.",
                  "practice": "1. (Easy) 30% rain forecast. High or low chance?\n2. (Easy) 8/10 topics studied. P(studied topic chosen)?\n3. (Medium) Bus on time 85/100 days. P(on time tomorrow)?\n4. (Medium) 180/200 seeds germinate. P(germinate)? Expect from 1,000 seeds?\n5. (Hard) Factory: 2% faulty. Buy 5 phones. Expected faulty? If you want <1 expected faulty, how many can you buy?",
                  "practice_answers": "1. Low\n2. 8/10 = 0.8\n3. 0.85\n4. 0.9; 900 seeds\n5. Expected 0.1 faulty in 5; buy up to 49 for <1 expected",
                  "summary": "* Probability helps make decisions\n* Use past data to predict future\n* Understand and manage risk\n* Expected value = P × number of trials",
                  "quiz": {
                    "question": "200 seeds, 180 germinate. P(germinate):",
                    "options": {
                      "A": "0.8",
                      "B": "0.85",
                      "C": "0.9",
                      "D": "0.95"
                    },
                    "answer": "C"
                  }
                }
              ],
              "previous_answers": "Unit 8 Quiz Answers: 1-B, 2-B, 3-C, 4-B, 5-C, 6-B, 7-A, 8-B, 9-B, 10-B",
              "unit_quiz": [
